window.NOTES_PROBLEMS=[{"id":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07#p-c1","u":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07.html#p-c1","l":"C1","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"cpu","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 01 to 07","q":"<p id=\"p-c1\"><strong>C1.</strong> How many processes exist in total (including the original) after this runs? Draw the process tree.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++)\n    fork();\n</code></pre>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>8 processes</strong> (2³). Every process that exists when <code>fork()</code> runs is duplicated, and the child continues the loop from the same <code>i</code>. Call the original P.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>At i = 0, P creates C1.</li>\n<li>At i = 1, P creates C2, and C1 creates C1a.</li>\n<li>At i = 2, P creates C3, C1 creates C1b, C2 creates C2a, and C1a creates C1a-i.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That gives 1 + 1 + 2 + 4 = 8.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07#p-c2","u":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07.html#p-c2","l":"C2","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"cpu","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 01 to 07","q":"<p id=\"p-c2\"><strong>C2.</strong> What does this print, and in what order? Could the order ever change?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">int x = 5;\nint rc = fork();\nif (rc == 0) {\n    x += 10;\n    printf(&quot;child %d\\n&quot;, x);\n} else {\n    wait(NULL);\n    x -= 1;\n    printf(&quot;parent %d\\n&quot;, x);\n}\n</code></pre>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> It prints <code>child 15</code>, then <code>parent 4</code>, <strong>always in that order</strong>. The parent calls <code>wait(NULL)</code> before printing, so it cannot print until the child has exited. The child's change to <code>x</code> does not affect the parent's <code>x</code>, because after <code>fork</code> each process has its own copy of the address space. The parent's 5 becomes 4.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07#p-c3","u":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07.html#p-c3","l":"C3","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"cpu","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 01 to 07","q":"<p id=\"p-c3\"><strong>C3.</strong> A program calls <code>execvp(&quot;ls&quot;, args)</code> and the next line is <code>printf(&quot;done\\n&quot;)</code>. When does &quot;done&quot; print?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Never, if <code>exec</code> succeeds.</strong> <code>exec</code> replaces the calling process's code, data, heap and stack with the new program, so the old program, including the <code>printf</code> line, no longer exists. <code>exec</code> only returns if it fails (for example, the file is not found), and then &quot;done&quot; prints.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07#p-c4","u":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07.html#p-c4","l":"C4","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"cpu","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 01 to 07","q":"<p id=\"p-c4\"><strong>C4.</strong> Explain, step by step, how a shell runs <code>ls &gt; out.txt</code> using <code>fork</code>, <code>close</code>, <code>open</code> and <code>exec</code>. Why is it important that fork and exec are separate calls?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> The steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The shell calls <code>fork()</code>.</li>\n<li>The child calls <code>close(1)</code>, which frees file descriptor 1 (standard output).</li>\n<li>The child calls <code>open(&quot;out.txt&quot;, O_CREAT | O_WRONLY | O_TRUNC, ...)</code>. <code>open</code> always returns the <strong>lowest free descriptor</strong>, which is now 1.</li>\n<li>The child calls <code>exec(&quot;ls&quot;, ...)</code>. <code>ls</code> writes to descriptor 1 as usual, but descriptor 1 is now the file.</li>\n<li>The parent shell calls <code>wait()</code>, then prints the next prompt.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Because fork and exec are separate, the child can change its own environment (open files, redirections, pipes) in the gap <em>after</em> <code>fork</code> and <em>before</em> <code>exec</code>, without touching the shell itself and without <code>ls</code> knowing anything about it.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07#p-c5","u":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07.html#p-c5","l":"C5","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"cpu","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 01 to 07","q":"<p id=\"p-c5\"><strong>C5.</strong> In limited direct execution, how does the OS get the CPU back from a process stuck in <code>while (1);</code>? During the switch that follows, two different register saves happen. Who does each one, and where are the registers saved?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> Through the <strong>timer interrupt</strong>. At boot, the OS sets up a hardware timer (a privileged operation) that interrupts every few milliseconds. When it fires, the CPU jumps into the OS's interrupt handler, whatever the process was doing, and the scheduler can switch to another process. The two register saves are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>By the hardware</strong>, at the moment of the interrupt: process A's <em>user</em> registers are saved onto A's <strong>kernel stack</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>By the OS</strong>, when it decides to switch: the switch routine (<code>swtch</code> in xv6) saves A's <em>kernel</em> registers into A's <strong>process structure</strong> (its PCB / <code>context</code>), and loads B's kernel registers from B's process structure.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The return-from-trap then restores B's user registers from B's kernel stack, and B resumes.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07#p-c6","u":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07.html#p-c6","l":"C6","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"cpu","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 01 to 07","q":"<p id=\"p-c6\"><strong>C6.</strong> Three jobs arrive as follows. Job A arrives at 0 and needs 30. Jobs B and C both arrive at 10; B needs 10 and C needs 25. For <strong>FIFO, SJF, STCF and RR</strong> (time slice 10), give the schedule, the average turnaround time and the average response time. For RR, assume jobs that arrive at the same moment a slice ends join the queue ahead of the job that was just preempted.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> A runs 0-30 if it is never preempted. B and C arrive at 10.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Policy</th>\n<th>Schedule</th>\n<th>Turnaround (A, B, C)</th>\n<th>Avg</th>\n<th>Response (A, B, C)</th>\n<th>Avg</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>FIFO</td>\n<td>A 0-30, B 30-40, C 40-65</td>\n<td>30, 30, 55</td>\n<td><strong>38.3</strong></td>\n<td>0, 20, 30</td>\n<td><strong>16.7</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SJF</td>\n<td>Same as FIFO</td>\n<td>30, 30, 55</td>\n<td><strong>38.3</strong></td>\n<td>0, 20, 30</td>\n<td><strong>16.7</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>STCF</td>\n<td>A 0-10, B 10-20, A 20-40, C 40-65</td>\n<td>40, 10, 55</td>\n<td><strong>35</strong></td>\n<td>0, 0, 30</td>\n<td><strong>10</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RR (10)</td>\n<td>A 0-10, B 10-20, C 20-30, A 30-40, C 40-50, A 50-60, C 60-65</td>\n<td>60, 10, 55</td>\n<td><strong>41.7</strong></td>\n<td>0, 0, 10</td>\n<td><strong>3.3</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Points to notice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SJF is no better than FIFO here.</strong> When B and C arrive, A is already running, and non-preemptive SJF cannot stop it.</li>\n<li><strong>STCF</strong> preempts A at t = 10, because B has 10 left and A has 20. At t = 20, A (20 left) beats C (25 left).</li>\n<li><strong>RR</strong> has the best response time but the worst turnaround, which is the trade-off from 1.4.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07#p-c7","u":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07.html#p-c7","l":"C7","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"cpu","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 01 to 07","q":"<p id=\"p-c7\"><strong>C7.</strong> (a) Which MLFQ rule stops a job from &quot;gaming&quot; the scheduler, and how did the old rules allow it? (b) What problem does the priority boost solve, and what goes wrong if the boost period S is too large or too small?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a) <strong>Rule 4</strong>: a job's allotment at a level counts its <em>total</em> CPU time there, however many times it gave up the CPU. The old Rule 4b let a job keep its priority if it gave up the CPU before its slice ended. A job could issue a pointless I/O at 99% of every slice and stay in the top queue forever.</p>\n<p>(b) The <strong>priority boost</strong> (Rule 5) fixes <strong>starvation</strong> (long jobs at the bottom get to run at least every S) and <strong>behaviour change</strong> (a job that becomes interactive gets back to the top). If S is too large, long jobs still starve between boosts. If S is too small, jobs are moved to the top so often that interactive jobs lose their advantage, and MLFQ behaves like plain round robin.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07#p-c8","u":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07.html#p-c8","l":"C8","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"cpu","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 01 to 07","q":"<p id=\"p-c8\"><strong>C8.</strong> Two CPU-bound processes run under CFS with sched_latency = 48 ms. A has nice 0 (weight 1024) and B has nice 5 (weight 335). Compute each one's time slice, and how much each one's vruntime grows during its slice. What do you notice?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> The slice is in proportion to the weight:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A: 48 × 1024 / (1024 + 335) = <strong>36.17 ms</strong></li>\n<li>B: 48 × 335 / 1359 = <strong>11.83 ms</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>vruntime grows by the time run × (1024 / weight):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A: 36.17 × 1024/1024 = <strong>36.17</strong></li>\n<li>B: 11.83 × 1024/335 = <strong>36.17</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>They are equal.</strong> The weights make vruntime grow <em>more slowly</em> for a higher-priority process, so when CFS keeps all vruntimes level, the higher-weight process gets proportionally more real CPU time. A gets about 75% of the CPU and B about 25%.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n1","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n1","l":"N1","s":"L08","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n1\"><strong>N1 (L08).</strong> A switch connects hosts H1, H2 and H3 within the network 192.168.10.x. Explain why the switch alone cannot let H2 communicate with a host on a completely different network, and name the device that makes this possible.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> A switch works at the <strong>data link layer (L2)</strong>. It forwards frames by <strong>MAC address</strong> between hosts on the same local network. It has no notion of IP networks or routes, so it cannot decide how to send a packet to a host outside 192.168.10.x. That needs a <strong>router</strong>, which works at the <strong>network layer (L3)</strong>: it reads the destination <strong>IP address</strong> and forwards the packet towards the destination network, hop by hop.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n2","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n2","l":"N2","s":"L08","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n2\"><strong>N2 (L08).</strong> Using the OSI model, explain why data sent by an application on Host 1 must pass down through Layers 7 to 1 before reaching the physical medium, and then back up through Layers 1 to 7 on Host 2, instead of going directly from application to application.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> Two applications on different hosts have no direct connection: the only thing that physically links the two machines is the medium at <strong>Layer 1</strong>. Each layer provides a service to the layer above by using the layer below. Each layer also talks <em>logically</em> to its peer layer on the other host, through the header it adds. So the data must go down the stack to reach the wire, and back up the stack on the other side, where each layer reads and removes its peer's header. This layering keeps each layer independent: the application does not need to know about routing, MAC addresses or signalling, and a layer's technology (say, Wi-Fi instead of Ethernet) can change without affecting the others.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n3","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n3","l":"N3","s":"L08","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n3\"><strong>N3 (L08).</strong> Describe encapsulation as data moves from the Application layer down to the Physical layer. Define a segment, a packet and a frame, and explain how decapsulation reverses the process at the receiving host.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> Going down the stack, each layer wraps what it receives in its own header:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The <strong>application</strong> produces a message.</li>\n<li>The <strong>transport layer</strong> adds a header with ports, sequence numbers and a checksum. The result is a <strong>segment</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>network layer</strong> adds an IP header with the source and destination IP addresses. The result is a <strong>packet</strong> (datagram).</li>\n<li>The <strong>data link layer</strong> adds a header with MAC addresses and a trailer with an error check. The result is a <strong>frame</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>physical layer</strong> sends the frame as bits.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>At the receiver, <strong>decapsulation</strong> reverses this. Each layer checks and removes its own header and hands the payload up to the right protocol above (using a type, protocol or port field), until the application receives the original message.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n4","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n4","l":"N4","s":"L08","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n4\"><strong>N4 (L08).</strong> Machine 234 sends data to Machine 345 across two routers. Explain the difference between hop-to-hop and end-to-end delivery here, and which layer is responsible for each.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> Per L08:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hop-to-hop</strong> delivery moves a frame from one node to the <em>next directly connected</em> node: 234 to the first router, router to router, and the last router to 345. This is the <strong>data link layer (L2)</strong>, using MAC addresses, which change at every hop.</li>\n<li><strong>End-to-end</strong> delivery gets the data all the way from 234 to 345 across those hops, choosing the route. This is the <strong>network layer (L3)</strong>, using IP addresses, which stay the same for the whole trip.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The transport layer (L4) then extends delivery from host-to-host to process-to-process, using ports.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n5","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n5","l":"N5","s":"L08","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n5\"><strong>N5 (L08).</strong> Using the example of sending one video-call frame from Host 1 to Host 2, describe two points in the journey where an <strong>OS mechanism</strong> (not a networking protocol) is essential, and what would go wrong at each without it.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> Any two of these, from L08's walk-through of sending one video frame:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>System call (trap) for <code>send()</code>.</strong> The application cannot touch the network card directly; it must trap into the kernel. Without this protection, any process could read or write the NIC and other processes' data.</li>\n<li><strong>Kernel buffers (memory management).</strong> The kernel copies the frame into OS-managed memory where TCP and IP headers are attached. Without them, the protocol stack would have nowhere to build packets or hold received data.</li>\n<li><strong>Interrupts from the NIC.</strong> Interrupts tell the OS that a transmission finished or a packet arrived. Without them, the OS would have to poll the NIC constantly, wasting CPU, or would never notice arriving data.</li>\n<li><strong>Process states and the scheduler.</strong> The receiving app is <strong>blocked</strong> on <code>recv()</code> and is moved to <strong>ready</strong> when data arrives. Without this, the app would have to spin while waiting, wasting the CPU.</li>\n<li><strong>Timers.</strong> A timer interrupt tells TCP when to retransmit a lost segment. Without it, a lost packet would never be noticed.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n6","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n6","l":"N6","s":"sheet Q1","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n6\"><strong>N6 (sheet Q1).</strong> A host sends a 5000-byte application message over TCP. TCP adds a 20-byte header; IP adds a 20-byte header to every packet or fragment; Ethernet adds a 14-byte header and a 4-byte trailer (18 bytes) to every frame. The Ethernet MTU limits each IP packet (header plus payload) to 1500 bytes.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Why does the 1500-byte MTU constrain the IP packet but not the Ethernet header and trailer? Why does a lower layer never need to understand the payload it is handed?</li>\n<li>(b) How big is the TCP segment handed to IP?</li>\n<li>(c) Does it fit in one IP packet? If not, give the minimum number of fragments and the exact size of each. (IP fragments the segment as plain bytes and knows nothing of the TCP header; every fragment needs its own 20-byte IP header.)</li>\n<li>(d) Each fragment goes in its own Ethernet frame. How many bytes are transmitted on the wire in total?</li>\n<li>(e) After IP reassembles the fragments, how does the transport layer demultiplex the segment to the right socket, and which header fields does it use? Why can this not happen fragment by fragment?</li>\n</ul>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a) The MTU limits <strong>what the link layer will carry as its payload</strong>, which is the IP packet. The Ethernet header and trailer are wrapped around that payload by the link layer itself, so they are outside the limit. Each layer treats what it is handed as an opaque string of bytes: it only needs <em>its own</em> header to do its job. This is what lets layers be designed and replaced independently.</p>\n<p>(b) 5000 + 20 = <strong>5020 bytes</strong>.</p>\n<p>(c) <strong>No.</strong> Each IP packet can carry at most 1500 − 20 = <strong>1480 bytes</strong> of payload. 5020 / 1480 = 3.39, so <strong>4 fragments</strong> are needed:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fragment</th>\n<th>Payload</th>\n<th>IP packet size</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1480 (includes the 20-byte TCP header)</td>\n<td>1500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>1480</td>\n<td>1500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>1480</td>\n<td>1500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>5020 − 3 × 1480 = 580</td>\n<td>600</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(1480 is a multiple of 8, as IP fragment offsets require.) Note that only fragment 1 contains the TCP header.</p>\n<p>(d) Each frame adds 18 bytes: 3 × 1518 + 618 = <strong>5172 bytes</strong> on the wire. That is 172 bytes of overhead on 5000 bytes of data: 20 (TCP) + 4 × 20 (IP) + 4 × 18 (Ethernet).</p>\n<p>(e) The IP layer reassembles the fragments using the <strong>Identification</strong>, <strong>Flags</strong> (more-fragments) and <strong>Fragment Offset</strong> fields, and its <strong>Protocol</strong> field (6) says to pass the result to TCP. TCP then demultiplexes using the <strong>4-tuple</strong>: source and destination IP (from the IP header) and <strong>source and destination port</strong> (from the TCP header). This cannot happen fragment by fragment, because:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Only the first fragment contains the TCP header, and so the ports. Fragments 2-4 are just bytes, with nothing to say which socket they belong to.</li>\n<li>The TCP checksum covers the whole segment, so it cannot be verified until every fragment has arrived.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n7","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n7","l":"N7","s":"sheet Q2","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n7\"><strong>N7 (sheet Q2).</strong> A TCP sender starts with EstimatedRTT = 100 ms, DevRTT = 10 ms, α = 0.125 and β = 0.25. It then measures SampleRTT = 140, 90 and 115 ms.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Compute EstimatedRTT, DevRTT and TimeoutInterval after each sample, to 3 decimal places.</li>\n<li>(b) Right after sample 3, a 4th segment is sent and its ACK arrives 172 ms later. Would the timer already have fired, causing an unnecessary retransmission?</li>\n<li>(c) In one sentence: why does TCP use 4 × DevRTT as the margin, rather than a fixed constant such as 20 ms?</li>\n</ul>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> The formulas are EstimatedRTT = 0.875 · EstimatedRTT + 0.125 · Sample, then DevRTT = 0.75 · DevRTT + 0.25 · |Sample − EstimatedRTT|, and Timeout = EstimatedRTT + 4 · DevRTT. Here DevRTT uses the <em>just-updated</em> EstimatedRTT, as in the Unit 2 worked example. (RFC 6298 updates DevRTT first, using the old estimate, which gives slightly different numbers; follow whichever order your exam uses.)</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sample</th>\n<th>SampleRTT</th>\n<th>EstimatedRTT</th>\n<th>DevRTT</th>\n<th>TimeoutInterval</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>140</td>\n<td>0.875·100 + 0.125·140 = <strong>105.000</strong></td>\n<td>0.75·10 + 0.25·35 = <strong>16.250</strong></td>\n<td>105 + 65 = <strong>170.000</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>90</td>\n<td>0.875·105 + 0.125·90 = <strong>103.125</strong></td>\n<td>0.75·16.25 + 0.25·13.125 = <strong>15.469</strong></td>\n<td>103.125 + 61.875 = <strong>165.000</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>115</td>\n<td>0.875·103.125 + 0.125·115 = <strong>104.609</strong></td>\n<td>0.75·15.469 + 0.25·10.391 = <strong>14.199</strong></td>\n<td>104.609 + 56.797 = <strong>161.406</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(b) <strong>Yes.</strong> The timeout is 161.406 ms, and the ACK arrives at 172 ms. The timer fires about 10.6 ms before the ACK arrives, so the sender retransmits a segment that was not actually lost.</p>\n<p>(c) DevRTT measures how much the RTT actually varies on this path, so the safety margin grows on jittery paths (avoiding spurious retransmissions) and shrinks on stable ones (so real losses are detected quickly), which no single fixed constant can do for every path.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n8","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n8","l":"N8","s":"sheet Q3","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n8\"><strong>N8 (sheet Q3).</strong> A TCP connection has MSS = 1000 bytes, a fixed RTT of 80 ms, cwnd = 1 MSS and ssthresh = 16 MSS at the start. One full window is sent and acknowledged every RTT.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Tabulate cwnd for rounds 1-9, marking slow start and congestion avoidance.</li>\n<li>(b) At the end of round 9, a loss is detected by three duplicate ACKs. Give the new ssthresh and cwnd.</li>\n<li>(c) Give cwnd for rounds 10, 11 and 12.</li>\n<li>(d) Approximate the throughput (cwnd / RTT) in Mbps at round 4 and at round 11.</li>\n<li>(e) If the loss at round 9 had been detected by a <strong>timeout</strong> instead, what would cwnd and ssthresh become, and which phase does TCP re-enter? Why does TCP treat the two loss signals differently?</li>\n</ul>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a)</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Round</th>\n<th>1</th>\n<th>2</th>\n<th>3</th>\n<th>4</th>\n<th>5</th>\n<th>6</th>\n<th>7</th>\n<th>8</th>\n<th>9</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>cwnd (MSS)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>16</td>\n<td>17</td>\n<td>18</td>\n<td>19</td>\n<td>20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Phase</td>\n<td>SS</td>\n<td>SS</td>\n<td>SS</td>\n<td>SS</td>\n<td>reaches ssthresh</td>\n<td>CA</td>\n<td>CA</td>\n<td>CA</td>\n<td>CA</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Slow start doubles cwnd every RTT until it reaches ssthresh = 16 in round 5. From then on, congestion avoidance adds 1 MSS per RTT.</p>\n<p>(b) Three duplicate ACKs trigger multiplicative decrease: <strong>ssthresh = 20 / 2 = 10</strong>, and <strong>cwnd = 10</strong> (fast recovery: no return to slow start).</p>\n<p>(c) Rounds 10, 11, 12: <strong>10, 11, 12</strong> (congestion avoidance).</p>\n<p>(d) Throughput ≈ cwnd × MSS / RTT.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Round 4: 8 × 1000 bytes × 8 bits / 0.08 s = 800,000 bit/s = <strong>0.8 Mbps</strong>.</li>\n<li>Round 11: 11 × 8000 bits / 0.08 s = <strong>1.1 Mbps</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(e) On a timeout: <strong>ssthresh = 10</strong>, <strong>cwnd = 1 MSS</strong>, and TCP re-enters <strong>slow start</strong>. The reason for the difference: three duplicate ACKs prove that later segments are still getting through, so the network is only mildly congested. A timeout means nothing is coming back at all, which suggests severe congestion, so TCP backs off much harder.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n9","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n9","l":"N9","s":"sheet Q4","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n9\"><strong>N9 (sheet Q4).</strong> Flow control limits a sender to at most Window bytes in flight (LastByteSent − LastByteAcked), and TCP's ACKs are cumulative.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) State the exact condition under which a sender with plenty of data must stop sending.</li>\n<li>(b) A sender has exactly W bytes in flight. The <strong>first</strong> of these segments is lost, but all the later ones arrive and are buffered. What ACK value does the receiver send back, and why does the sender's usable window not grow at all?</li>\n<li>(c) The lost segment is then retransmitted and arrives. What happens to the next ACK, and why can this one retransmission release a large burst of new data?</li>\n<li>(d) In one sentence: why can one early loss have an outsized effect on a flow-control-limited sender's throughput?</li>\n</ul>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a) The sender must stop when <strong>LastByteSent − LastByteAcked ≥ W</strong>, that is, when a full window of unacknowledged data is in flight.</p>\n<p>(b) Every segment that arrives after the gap produces an ACK carrying the <strong>same</strong> number: the first byte of the lost segment (the next byte the receiver expects in order). These are <strong>duplicate ACKs</strong>. Because ACKs are cumulative, the receiver cannot acknowledge any byte beyond the gap, even though it has buffered them. LastByteAcked therefore does not move, the sender still has W bytes in flight, and it cannot send anything new. (Three duplicate ACKs will trigger fast retransmit.)</p>\n<p>(c) Once the missing segment arrives, the gap is filled, and the receiver's next ACK <strong>jumps</strong> to the byte after the last buffered segment, acknowledging all W bytes at once. LastByteAcked advances by W in one step, the in-flight amount drops to zero, and the sender may immediately send a <strong>whole new window</strong> of data as a burst.</p>\n<p>(d) Because cumulative ACKs cannot move past a hole, one early loss freezes the entire window until the retransmission arrives (a timeout, or three duplicate ACKs plus a round trip), so throughput drops to almost zero for that time even though nearly all the data was delivered.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n10","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n10","l":"N10","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n10\"><strong>N10.</strong> Compute the 16-bit Internet checksum of the three words 0xF0F0, 0x1234 and 0x8001. Show how the receiver checks it. Give an example of a two-bit error the checksum would miss.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> Add the words with 16-bit ones'-complement addition (any carry out of the top bit is added back in at the bottom):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>0xF0F0 + 0x1234 = 0x10324. Wrap the carry around: 0x0324 + 1 = <strong>0x0325</strong>.</li>\n<li>0x0325 + 0x8001 = <strong>0x8326</strong> (no carry).</li>\n<li>The checksum is the bitwise complement: ~0x8326 = <strong>0x7CD9</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The receiver adds all four words, including the checksum: 0x8326 + 0x7CD9 = <strong>0xFFFF</strong>. All ones means no error was detected; any 0 bit means an error.</p>\n<p><strong>A missed error:</strong> if the lowest bit of 0xF0F0 flips from 0 to 1 (giving 0xF0F1) and the lowest bit of 0x8001 flips from 1 to 0 (giving 0x8000), the sum is unchanged, so the checksum still passes. Two errors in the same bit position, in opposite directions, cancel out.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n11","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n11","l":"N11","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n11\"><strong>N11.</strong> Two browsers on different machines both connect to a web server on port 80. (a) How does the server's OS deliver each arriving segment to the right connection socket? (b) How would this differ for a UDP server on port 53? (c) List the system calls a TCP server makes, in order, up to the point where it can read a client's request, and say what <code>accept</code> returns.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a) A TCP connection socket is identified by the <strong>4-tuple</strong> (source IP, source port, destination IP, destination port). Both clients' segments have destination port 80, but their source IPs and ports differ, so the OS finds a different connection socket for each. The listening socket on port 80 only receives new connection requests (SYNs).</p>\n<p>(b) A UDP socket is identified only by <strong>(destination IP, destination port)</strong>, so datagrams from all clients arrive at the <strong>same</strong> socket. The server uses the source address returned by <code>recvfrom</code> to know where to send each reply.</p>\n<p>(c) <code>socket()</code> → <code>bind()</code> (attach to port 80) → <code>listen()</code> → <code>accept()</code> → <code>read()</code>/<code>recv()</code>. <code>accept</code> blocks until a client connects, then returns a <strong>new file descriptor</strong>: a connected socket for that one client. The listening socket stays open so the server can accept more clients.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m1","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m1","l":"M1","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m1\"><strong>M1.</strong> A process has base = 16384 and bounds = 4096 (bounds is the size). Translate the virtual addresses 0, 1000, 4096 and 5000, or say which ones fault.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> A virtual address is legal only if it is <strong>less than</strong> bounds. Then PA = VA + base.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>VA</th>\n<th>Check</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>0 &lt; 4096</td>\n<td>16384</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1000</td>\n<td>1000 &lt; 4096</td>\n<td>17384</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4096</td>\n<td>4096 is not &lt; 4096</td>\n<td><strong>Fault</strong> (the last legal address is 4095)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5000</td>\n<td>5000 ≥ 4096</td>\n<td><strong>Fault</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m2","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m2","l":"M2","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m2\"><strong>M2.</strong> A system uses a 14-bit virtual address. The top 2 bits pick the segment: 00 = code, 01 = heap, 11 = stack. The maximum segment size is therefore 4KB.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Segment</th>\n<th>Base</th>\n<th>Size</th>\n<th>Grows</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Code (00)</td>\n<td>32K</td>\n<td>2K</td>\n<td>Positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heap (01)</td>\n<td>34K</td>\n<td>3K</td>\n<td>Positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stack (11)</td>\n<td>28K</td>\n<td>2K</td>\n<td>Negative</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Translate the virtual addresses 1500, 5000, 7500, 16000 and 13000, or say which ones fault.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> The top 2 bits pick the segment, so each segment covers 4096 virtual addresses: 0-4095 is segment 00, 4096-8191 is 01, and 12288-16383 is 11. Subtract the segment's start to get the offset.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>VA</th>\n<th>Segment, offset</th>\n<th>Check</th>\n<th>Physical address</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1500</td>\n<td>Code, 1500</td>\n<td>1500 &lt; 2K</td>\n<td>32768 + 1500 = <strong>34268</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5000</td>\n<td>Heap, 5000 − 4096 = 904</td>\n<td>904 &lt; 3K</td>\n<td>34816 + 904 = <strong>35720</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7500</td>\n<td>Heap, 3404</td>\n<td>3404 ≥ 3072</td>\n<td><strong>Fault</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16000</td>\n<td>Stack, 16000 − 12288 = 3712</td>\n<td>See below</td>\n<td><strong>28288</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13000</td>\n<td>Stack, 712</td>\n<td>See below</td>\n<td><strong>Fault</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The stack grows downward, so its offset is converted to a negative one: negative offset = offset − max segment size (4096).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>For 16000: 3712 − 4096 = −384. |−384| ≤ 2048, so it is legal. PA = 28672 − 384 = <strong>28288</strong>.</li>\n<li>For 13000: 712 − 4096 = −3384. |−3384| &gt; 2048, so it is beyond the stack's 2K: <strong>fault</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m3","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m3","l":"M3","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m3\"><strong>M3.</strong> A free list holds chunks of 20, 10, 40 and 15 bytes, in that order. A request for 12 bytes arrives. Which chunk does each of best fit, worst fit and first fit choose, and how big is the leftover piece? Which strategy tends to leave many tiny unusable pieces, and why?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Strategy</th>\n<th>Chooses</th>\n<th>Leftover</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Best fit (smallest that fits)</td>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Worst fit (largest)</td>\n<td>40</td>\n<td>28</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>First fit (first that fits)</td>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>8</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Best fit</strong> tends to leave many tiny pieces: by choosing the closest fit, it makes the leftover as small as possible, and these slivers are often too small for any later request. Worst fit tries to keep leftovers large, but in practice it also fragments badly, and it must search the whole list.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m4","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m4","l":"M4","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m4\"><strong>M4.</strong> A buddy allocator manages 128KB. (a) Show the splits for a 10KB request. How much internal fragmentation is there? (b) A 30KB request follows. Where does it go? (c) Why is coalescing cheap in a buddy system?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a) 10KB rounds up to the next power of two, 16KB. The allocator splits 128 into 64 + 64, then one 64 into 32 + 32, then one 32 into 16 + 16, and returns one 16KB block. Internal fragmentation is 16 − 10 = <strong>6KB</strong>.</p>\n<p>(b) 30KB rounds up to 32KB. There is a free 32KB block left over from the second split, so it is used directly, with 2KB of internal fragmentation. The free blocks are now one 16KB and one 64KB.</p>\n<p>(c) A block's buddy is found by flipping a single bit of its address (the bit that matches the block's size). When a block is freed, the allocator checks that one buddy; if it is also free, the two merge, and this repeats up the levels.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m5","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m5","l":"M5","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m5\"><strong>M5.</strong> (a) A 32-bit address space with 8KB pages and 4-byte PTEs: how many bits are in the VPN and offset, and how big is a linear page table? (b) What would 100 such processes cost?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a) 8KB = 2¹³, so the <strong>offset is 13 bits</strong> and the <strong>VPN is 32 − 13 = 19 bits</strong>. That is 2¹⁹ = 524,288 PTEs × 4 bytes = <strong>2MB</strong> per process.</p>\n<p>(b) 100 processes need <strong>200MB</strong> of page tables. That is why 3.7's smaller-table techniques exist.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m6","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m6","l":"M6","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m6\"><strong>M6.</strong> A 1KB address space uses 64-byte pages. The page table is: VPN 0 → PFN 5, VPN 1 → invalid, VPN 2 → PFN 9, VPN 3 → PFN 1. Translate virtual addresses 165, 112 and 200.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> A 1KB address space has 10-bit addresses. 64-byte pages mean a 6-bit offset, leaving a 4-bit VPN. VPN = VA / 64 and offset = VA mod 64.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>VA</th>\n<th>VPN, offset</th>\n<th>PTE</th>\n<th>Physical address</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>165</td>\n<td>2, 37</td>\n<td>PFN 9</td>\n<td>9 × 64 + 37 = <strong>613</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>112</td>\n<td>1, 48</td>\n<td>Invalid</td>\n<td><strong>Fault</strong>: the OS usually kills the process</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>200</td>\n<td>3, 8</td>\n<td>PFN 1</td>\n<td>1 × 64 + 8 = <strong>72</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m7","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m7","l":"M7","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m7\"><strong>M7.</strong> An array of 12 four-byte ints starts at virtual address 120, and the page size is 32 bytes. A loop reads a[0] to a[11] once, starting with an empty TLB. How many TLB hits and misses are there, and what is the hit rate? What would happen if the loop ran a second time straight away? Which kind of locality does each pass show?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> The ints sit at 120, 124, ..., 164. Page = address / 32:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Page 3 (96-127) holds a[0] and a[1]: one miss, then one hit.</li>\n<li>Page 4 (128-159) holds a[2] to a[9]: one miss, then seven hits.</li>\n<li>Page 5 (160-191) holds a[10] and a[11]: one miss, then one hit.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That is <strong>3 misses and 9 hits, a 75% hit rate</strong>. These hits come from <strong>spatial locality</strong>: neighbouring elements share a page.</p>\n<p>On an immediate second pass, all three translations are still in the TLB (assuming it holds at least three entries), so all 12 accesses hit. Those hits come from <strong>temporal locality</strong>: the same pages are used again soon.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m8","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m8","l":"M8","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m8\"><strong>M8.</strong> A TLB lookup takes 2 ns and a memory access takes 100 ns. The page table is a single-level linear table, and the TLB hit rate is 95%. What is the effective memory-access time? What would it be with no TLB at all?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>On a TLB hit: 2 + 100 = 102 ns.</li>\n<li>On a TLB miss: 2 + 100 (fetch the PTE) + 100 (fetch the data) = 202 ns.</li>\n<li>EAT = 0.95 × 102 + 0.05 × 202 = 96.9 + 10.1 = <strong>107 ns</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>With no TLB, every access needs the PTE and then the data: <strong>200 ns</strong>, nearly twice as slow.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m9","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m9","l":"M9","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m9\"><strong>M9.</strong> Use the multi-level setup from 3.7: a 14-bit address space, 64-byte pages and 4-byte PTEs, so one page holds 16 PTEs.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Split VA 0x1234 into page-directory index, page-table index and offset.</li>\n<li>(b) Suppose PDE[4] is valid with PFN 0x20, and entry 8 of that page of the page table is valid with PFN 0x31. What is the physical address?</li>\n<li>(c) How many memory accesses does this take on a TLB miss?</li>\n<li>(d) For a 48-bit address space with 4KB pages and 8-byte PTEs, how many levels are needed if every piece of the page table must fit in one page?</li>\n</ul>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a) 0x1234 in 14 bits is <code>01 0010 0011 0100</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The offset is the low 6 bits: <code>110100</code> = <strong>52</strong>.</li>\n<li>The VPN is the high 8 bits: <code>01001000</code> = 72.</li>\n<li>The page-directory index is the top 4 bits of the VPN: <code>0100</code> = <strong>4</strong>.</li>\n<li>The page-table index is the low 4 bits of the VPN: <code>1000</code> = <strong>8</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(b) PA = (PFN &lt;&lt; 6) | offset = (0x31 &lt;&lt; 6) + 52 = 0xC40 + 0x34 = <strong>0xC74</strong> (3188).</p>\n<p>(c) <strong>3 memory accesses</strong>: the page-directory entry, the page-table entry, and the data itself.</p>\n<p>(d) A 4KB page holds 4096 / 8 = 512 PTEs, so each level resolves 9 bits. The VPN is 48 − 12 = 36 bits, and 36 / 9 = <strong>4 levels</strong>. This is exactly x86-64's four-level layout.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m10","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m10","l":"M10","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m10\"><strong>M10.</strong> With 3 frames, and starting empty, run the reference string <strong>7, 0, 1, 2, 0, 3, 0, 4, 2, 3</strong> under OPT, FIFO and LRU. Give the number of hits for each. How many of the misses are compulsory (cold-start) misses?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> All three policies start with 3 compulsory misses (7, 0, 1).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Ref</th>\n<th>FIFO</th>\n<th>Frames</th>\n<th>LRU</th>\n<th>Frames (least recent first)</th>\n<th>OPT</th>\n<th>Frames</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>7 0</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>7 0</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>7 0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>7 0 1</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>7 0 1</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>7 0 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>M, evict 7</td>\n<td>0 1 2</td>\n<td>M, evict 7</td>\n<td>0 1 2</td>\n<td>M, evict 7 (never used again)</td>\n<td>0 1 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td><strong>H</strong></td>\n<td>0 1 2</td>\n<td><strong>H</strong></td>\n<td>1 2 0</td>\n<td><strong>H</strong></td>\n<td>0 1 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>M, evict 0</td>\n<td>1 2 3</td>\n<td>M, evict 1</td>\n<td>2 0 3</td>\n<td>M, evict 1 (never used again)</td>\n<td>0 2 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>M, evict 1</td>\n<td>2 3 0</td>\n<td><strong>H</strong></td>\n<td>2 3 0</td>\n<td><strong>H</strong></td>\n<td>0 2 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>M, evict 2</td>\n<td>3 0 4</td>\n<td>M, evict 2</td>\n<td>3 0 4</td>\n<td>M, evict 0 (never used again)</td>\n<td>2 3 4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>M, evict 3</td>\n<td>0 4 2</td>\n<td>M, evict 3</td>\n<td>0 4 2</td>\n<td><strong>H</strong></td>\n<td>2 3 4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>M, evict 0</td>\n<td>4 2 3</td>\n<td>M, evict 0</td>\n<td>4 2 3</td>\n<td><strong>H</strong></td>\n<td>2 3 4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Hits: OPT 4, LRU 2, FIFO 1.</strong> There are 6 distinct pages, so 6 misses are compulsory. OPT's 6 misses are <em>all</em> compulsory: no policy could do better on this string.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m11","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m11","l":"M11","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m11\"><strong>M11.</strong> Four frames hold pages A, B, C and D, with use bits 1, 0, 1, 1. The clock hand points at A. Page E is requested, and then page F. Which page is evicted each time, and what are the use bits and hand position afterwards?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Request E.</strong> A's use bit is 1: clear it to 0 and move on. B's use bit is 0: <strong>evict B</strong>. E goes into B's frame with use bit 1, and the hand moves to C. The state is now A 0, E 1, C 1, D 1, hand at C.</p>\n<p><strong>Request F.</strong> C is 1, so clear it and move on. D is 1, so clear it and move on. A is 0: <strong>evict A</strong>. F goes into A's frame with use bit 1, and the hand moves to E. The state is now F 1, E 1, C 0, D 0, hand at E.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m12","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m12","l":"M12","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m12\"><strong>M12.</strong> Memory takes 100 ns and a disk access takes 10 ms. What is the AMAT with a hit rate of 99.9%, and with 99.99%? What does this say about how often a system can afford to page?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> AMAT = T_M + P_miss × T_D.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>At 99.9%: 100 ns + 0.001 × 10 ms = 100 ns + 10 µs = <strong>10.1 µs</strong>, about 100 times slower than memory.</li>\n<li>At 99.99%: 100 ns + 1 µs = <strong>1.1 µs</strong>, still 11 times slower.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Even a tiny miss rate dominates, because disk is 100,000 times slower than memory. A system can only afford to page very rarely: in practice, each process's working set must fit in memory.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m13","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m13","l":"M13","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m13\"><strong>M13.</strong> (a) What is thrashing, and what are two ways an OS can respond to it? (b) Why can LRU never suffer from Belady's anomaly while FIFO can? (c) Why does a clean page cost less to evict than a dirty one?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a) <strong>Thrashing</strong> is when the working sets of the running processes add up to more than physical memory, so the system spends nearly all its time paging and does little useful work. Two responses:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Admission control</strong>: run fewer processes (suspend some) so the rest fit in memory.</li>\n<li><strong>An out-of-memory killer</strong> (as in Linux): kill a memory-hungry process.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(b) LRU has the <strong>stack property</strong>: with N + 1 frames, the pages in memory are always a superset of those with N frames, so adding a frame can never add a miss. FIFO does not have this property, which is why adding a frame can make it worse.</p>\n<p>(c) A clean page is identical to its copy on disk, so it can simply be dropped. A dirty page must first be <strong>written back to disk</strong>, which costs an extra I/O.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m14","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m14","l":"M14","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m14\"><strong>M14.</strong> In xv6 (Sv39): (a) why are text pages mapped without the W bit? (b) What is the guard page below the user stack for, and why does it lack the U bit? (c) What does <code>satp</code> hold, and when does it change?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a) So that a program cannot overwrite its own code, whether by a bug or by an attack. A write to a text page causes a page fault.</p>\n<p>(b) If the stack overflows, it runs into the guard page, which has no U bit, so user code cannot access it. The result is a <strong>page fault</strong>, instead of silently overwriting the memory below the stack.</p>\n<p>(c) <code>satp</code> holds the physical page number of the <strong>root page table</strong> of the current address space (plus the mode, Sv39). It changes whenever the CPU switches address space. In xv6, that happens on every trap into and out of the kernel (in the trampoline code), and when the scheduler switches to another process.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s1","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s1","l":"S1","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s1\"><strong>S1.</strong> Two threads each run <code>counter++</code> three times, with no lock. counter starts at 0. What are the largest and the smallest possible final values? Give the interleaving that produces the smallest one.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> The largest is <strong>6</strong>: every increment runs without interruption. The smallest is <strong>2</strong>. Here is how:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>T1 loads 0 into its register, then is interrupted.</li>\n<li>T2 runs two complete increments: counter = 2.</li>\n<li>T1 adds 1 to its stale 0 and stores 1. <strong>T2's two increments are wiped out.</strong></li>\n<li>T2 loads 1 for its third increment, then is interrupted.</li>\n<li>T1 runs its last two increments in full: counter = 3.</li>\n<li>T2 adds 1 to its stale 1 and stores <strong>2</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The result cannot be 1. Every store writes (value loaded) + 1, so once any store has happened, memory holds at least 1. The final value comes from the last store, which is some thread's <em>last</em> increment. That increment loaded its value after the same thread's first increment had already stored, so it loaded at least 1 and stored at least 2.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s2","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s2","l":"S2","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s2\"><strong>S2.</strong> In the code below, which of <code>g</code>, <code>s</code>, <code>local</code>, <code>p</code> and <code>*p</code> are shared between two threads that both run <code>work()</code>?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">int g = 0;\nvoid *work(void *arg) {\n    static int s = 0;\n    int local = 0;\n    int *p = malloc(sizeof(int));\n    ...\n}\n</code></pre>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Variable</th>\n<th>Shared?</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>g</code></td>\n<td><strong>Yes</strong></td>\n<td>Globals live in the data segment, and there is one copy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>s</code></td>\n<td><strong>Yes</strong></td>\n<td>A <code>static</code> local also lives in the data segment, not on the stack</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>local</code></td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Each thread has it on its own stack</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>p</code></td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>The pointer variable itself is on each thread's stack</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>*p</code></td>\n<td>Not in practice</td>\n<td>The heap is shared memory, but each thread mallocs its own block. It becomes shared only if one thread passes the pointer to another</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s3","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s3","l":"S3","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s3\"><strong>S3.</strong> Show an interleaving in which the plain-flag lock from 4.2 (test with a load, then set with a store) lets two threads into the critical section. Then explain exactly why <code>TestAndSet</code> closes that gap.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> The bad interleaving, starting with flag = 0:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>T1 tests the flag and sees 0.</li>\n<li>An interrupt switches to T2. T2 tests, sees 0, and sets the flag to 1, entering the critical section.</li>\n<li>An interrupt switches back to T1, which sets the flag to 1 and also enters.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The problem is the gap between the test and the set. <code>TestAndSet</code> does both in <strong>one atomic instruction</strong>, so there is no gap. Whichever thread executes it first gets back 0 and leaves 1 behind; every other thread gets back 1 and keeps spinning.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s4","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s4","l":"S4","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s4\"><strong>S4.</strong> Four threads share one CPU and a test-and-set spin lock. The holder is preempted just after taking the lock. Roughly how much CPU time is wasted before the holder runs again, and why? How do <code>yield</code> and the queue lock each improve this?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> On one CPU, each of the other three threads gets scheduled in turn and spins for its <strong>whole time slice</strong>, because the lock cannot be released while its holder is not running. That is roughly <strong>3 time slices wasted</strong> for each time the holder is preempted (more if the scheduler is unlucky).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>With <strong>yield</strong>, each waiter gives up the CPU as soon as it finds the lock held. That costs 3 context switches instead of 3 slices, but it still has overhead and starvation is still possible.</li>\n<li>With the <strong>queue lock</strong>, waiters sleep and are not scheduled at all until the holder unparks them, so no time is wasted.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s5","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s5","l":"S5","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s5\"><strong>S5.</strong> A ticket lock starts with ticket = 0 and turn = 0. T1, T2 and T3 call <code>lock()</code> in that order, then T1 calls <code>unlock()</code>, then T4 calls <code>lock()</code>, then T2 calls <code>unlock()</code>. After each step, give ticket, turn, each thread's <code>myturn</code>, and who holds the lock.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>ticket</th>\n<th>turn</th>\n<th>myturn</th>\n<th>Holder</th>\n<th>Spinning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Start</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>none</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T1 lock</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>T1 = 0</td>\n<td>T1</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T2 lock</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>T2 = 1</td>\n<td>T1</td>\n<td>T2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T3 lock</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>T3 = 2</td>\n<td>T1</td>\n<td>T2, T3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T1 unlock</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><strong>T2</strong></td>\n<td>T3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T4 lock</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>T4 = 3</td>\n<td>T2</td>\n<td>T3, T4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T2 unlock</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><strong>T3</strong></td>\n<td>T4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Threads are served strictly in ticket order. That is what makes the ticket lock fair.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s6","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s6","l":"S6","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s6\"><strong>S6.</strong> In the queue lock with <code>park</code>/<code>unpark</code>: (a) What would go wrong if a thread called <code>park()</code> <em>before</em> releasing the guard? (b) Describe the wakeup/waiting race that remains, and how <code>setpark()</code> fixes it.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a) The thread would go to sleep <strong>holding the guard</strong>. When the lock holder calls <code>unlock()</code>, it spins forever trying to take the guard, so it can never unpark anyone. Everything stops.</p>\n<p>(b) The race:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A thread adds itself to the queue and releases the guard.</li>\n<li>It is preempted just <em>before</em> calling <code>park()</code>.</li>\n<li>The lock holder runs <code>unlock()</code> and calls <code>unpark()</code> on it. This has no effect, because the thread is not asleep yet.</li>\n<li>The thread runs again, calls <code>park()</code>, and <strong>sleeps forever</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>With <strong><code>setpark()</code></strong>, the thread announces &quot;I am about to park&quot; <em>before</em> releasing the guard. If an <code>unpark()</code> arrives between that announcement and the <code>park()</code>, the <code>park()</code> returns immediately instead of sleeping.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s7","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s7","l":"S7","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s7\"><strong>S7.</strong> Low-priority thread L holds a spin lock. High-priority thread H wakes up and tries to take it. Explain what happens on one CPU, and how priority inheritance fixes it.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> H has the higher priority, so the scheduler always runs H, and H spins on the lock. L never gets the CPU, so it never finishes its critical section and never releases the lock. The system hangs, even though nothing is technically deadlocked. With <strong>priority inheritance</strong>, while H waits for a lock that L holds, L temporarily runs at H's priority. L gets the CPU, finishes, releases the lock, and drops back to its own priority, and then H proceeds.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s8","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s8","l":"S8","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s8\"><strong>S8.</strong> Is this code correct? If not, give the interleaving that breaks it and fix it.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">// waiting thread                      // signalling thread\npthread_mutex_lock(&amp;m);                ready = 1;\nwhile (!ready)                         pthread_cond_signal(&amp;c);\n    pthread_cond_wait(&amp;c, &amp;m);\npthread_mutex_unlock(&amp;m);\n</code></pre>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Not correct: there is a lost wakeup</strong>, because the signaller does not take the lock.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The waiter locks m and sees <code>ready == 0</code>. Just before it calls <code>wait</code>, it is interrupted.</li>\n<li>The signaller sets <code>ready = 1</code> and signals. Nobody is waiting yet, so the signal is lost.</li>\n<li>The waiter calls <code>wait</code> and <strong>sleeps forever</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fix:</strong> the signaller must hold the same mutex:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">pthread_mutex_lock(&amp;m);\nready = 1;\npthread_cond_signal(&amp;c);\npthread_mutex_unlock(&amp;m);\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now the signaller cannot change <code>ready</code> between the waiter's check and its sleep, because the waiter holds m until <code>wait</code> has put it to sleep.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s9","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s9","l":"S9","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s9\"><strong>S9.</strong> Producer/consumer already uses two CVs (<code>empty</code> and <code>fill</code>). Do the waits still need <code>while</code>, or would <code>if</code> now be safe? Give a scenario to justify your answer.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong><code>while</code> is still needed.</strong> Two CVs make sure a signal wakes the right <em>kind</em> of thread, but not that the condition still holds when the woken thread runs. A scenario:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The buffer is empty, and Tc1 is asleep on <code>fill</code>.</li>\n<li>The producer puts an item and signals <code>fill</code>, so Tc1 becomes ready.</li>\n<li>Before Tc1 runs, a newly arriving consumer Tc2 gets the mutex, sees count == 1, and takes the item.</li>\n<li>Tc1 finally returns from <code>wait</code>. With <code>if</code>, it calls <code>get()</code> on an empty buffer.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Spurious wakeups are a second reason. Under Mesa semantics, always use <code>while</code>.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s10","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s10","l":"S10","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s10\"><strong>S10.</strong> For each, give the semaphore's initial value: (a) thread B's line <code>b1</code> must run after thread A's line <code>a1</code>; (b) at most 5 threads may hold a database connection at once; (c) a plain mutex; (d) the <code>full</code> semaphore of a bounded buffer with 8 slots; (e) its <code>empty</code> semaphore.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) <strong>0</strong>. (b) <strong>5</strong>. (c) <strong>1</strong>. (d) <code>full</code> = <strong>0</strong>. (e) <code>empty</code> = <strong>8</strong>.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s11","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s11","l":"S11","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s11\"><strong>S11.</strong> A semaphore starts at 2. T1, T2, T3, T4 and T5 call <code>sem_wait</code> in that order, then T1 and T2 each call <code>sem_post</code>. After each call, give the value and the list of waiting threads.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Call</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n<th>Waiting</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Start</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T1 wait</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T2 wait</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T3 wait</td>\n<td>−1</td>\n<td>T3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T4 wait</td>\n<td>−2</td>\n<td>T3, T4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T5 wait</td>\n<td>−3</td>\n<td>T3, T4, T5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T1 post</td>\n<td>−2</td>\n<td>T4, T5 (T3 woken)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T2 post</td>\n<td>−1</td>\n<td>T5 (T4 woken)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>At every step, a negative value equals minus the number of waiting threads.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s12","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s12","l":"S12","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s12\"><strong>S12.</strong> In the bounded buffer with semaphores: (a) does the order of the two <code>sem_post</code> calls in the producer matter? (b) Does the order of the two <code>sem_wait</code> calls matter? Explain each.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a) <strong>No.</strong> <code>sem_post</code> never blocks, so both posts always complete, in either order. Posting the mutex first just ends the critical section slightly sooner.</p>\n<p>(b) <strong>Yes.</strong> If a thread takes the mutex first and then waits on <code>full</code> or <code>empty</code>, it can go to sleep while holding the mutex. The thread that would wake it then blocks on the mutex, and both are stuck: the deadlock from 4.4. Always wait on <code>full</code>/<code>empty</code> first, then take the mutex.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s13","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s13","l":"S13","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s13\"><strong>S13.</strong> Using semaphores only, make three threads print A, B and C in exactly that order every time, whatever order they are scheduled in. Then make two threads print ABABAB... forever.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> For A, then B, then C: use two semaphores, <code>s1</code> and <code>s2</code>, both initialized to 0.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">// Thread A            // Thread B            // Thread C\nprintf(&quot;A&quot;);           sem_wait(&amp;s1);         sem_wait(&amp;s2);\nsem_post(&amp;s1);         printf(&quot;B&quot;);           printf(&quot;C&quot;);\n                       sem_post(&amp;s2);\n</code></pre>\n<p>B cannot print until A has posted s1, and C cannot print until B has posted s2, whatever order the threads start in.</p>\n<p>For ABAB...: <code>sA</code> starts at 1 (A goes first) and <code>sB</code> starts at 0.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">// Thread A                       // Thread B\nwhile (1) {                       while (1) {\n    sem_wait(&amp;sA);                    sem_wait(&amp;sB);\n    printf(&quot;A&quot;);                      printf(&quot;B&quot;);\n    sem_post(&amp;sB);                    sem_post(&amp;sA);\n}                                 }\n</code></pre>\n<p>Each thread hands the turn to the other.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s14","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s14","l":"S14","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s14\"><strong>S14.</strong> For the semaphore reader-writer lock: R1 holds a read lock, W calls <code>acquire_writelock</code>, then R2 calls <code>acquire_readlock</code>. What happens to W and R2, and what are <code>readers</code> and the value of <code>writelock</code>? Why is this unfair, and what is the general idea of the fix?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> While R1 is reading, readers = 1 and writelock = 0, because R1, as the first reader, took it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>W calls <code>sem_wait(&amp;writelock)</code>: the value goes to −1, and <strong>W sleeps</strong>.</li>\n<li>R2 takes <code>lock</code>, increments readers to 2, and, because it is not the first reader, does not touch writelock. <strong>R2 enters immediately.</strong></li>\n<li>Now readers = 2, writelock = −1, and W is still waiting.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is unfair: W arrived before R2, but R2 got in first. W must wait until readers drops all the way to 0, and if readers keep arriving, that may never happen. The fix is to make new readers queue behind a waiting writer, with a turnstile semaphore that the writer holds while it waits (LBoS 4.2.3).</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s15","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s15","l":"S15","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s15\"><strong>S15.</strong> Dining philosophers: (a) Does it also prevent deadlock if every <em>even-numbered</em> philosopher picks up the right fork first? (b) A different fix lets at most four philosophers try to eat at once, using a semaphore initialized to 4. Why does that prevent deadlock?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a) <strong>Yes.</strong> With even philosophers taking the right fork first, P0 and P1 both reach for f1 first, and P2 and P3 both reach for f3 first. In each pair, the one who loses holds <strong>nothing</strong>. So the five philosophers can never all be holding one fork each, and the circular wait cannot close. In general, any arrangement where not everyone reaches in the same direction breaks the cycle.</p>\n<p>(b) With at most 4 philosophers at the table and 5 forks, even if all 4 hold one fork, one fork is still free. One of the seated philosophers is next to it, so that philosopher can take it, eat, and release both forks. Someone can always make progress, so there is no deadlock. This is the multiplex pattern (LBoS 4.4 calls the semaphore the &quot;footman&quot;).</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s16","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s16","l":"S16","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s16\"><strong>S16.</strong> For each technique, name the deadlock condition it removes: (a) always locking in address order; (b) taking a global lock around all lock acquisitions; (c) <code>trylock</code> that releases what it holds on failure; (d) a lock-free counter using compare-and-swap.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) <strong>Circular wait</strong>. (b) <strong>Hold and wait</strong>. (c) <strong>No preemption</strong> (OSTEP's classification). Tutorial 6 files it under hold and wait, since after releasing, the thread never waits while holding a lock; both views are reasonable. (d) <strong>Mutual exclusion</strong>.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s17","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s17","l":"S17","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s17\"><strong>S17.</strong> T1 holds A and waits for B. T2 holds B and waits for C. T3 holds C and is running, and will not request anything else. Is the system deadlocked? What if T3 then requests A?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Not deadlocked.</strong> T3 is not waiting for anything, so it will finish and release C. Then T2 gets C, finishes, and releases B, and then T1 gets B. There is a chain, but no cycle. If T3 then requests A, there is a cycle: T1 waits for T2 (B), T2 waits for T3 (C), and T3 waits for T1 (A). All four conditions now hold: <strong>deadlock</strong>.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s18","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s18","l":"S18","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s18\"><strong>S18.</strong> There are 12 units of a resource. T0 has maximum 10 and holds 5; T1 has maximum 4 and holds 2; T2 has maximum 9 and holds 3. Is this state safe under the Banker's algorithm? Explain.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> The free units are 12 − (5 + 2 + 3) = <strong>2</strong>. The remaining needs are T0: 5, T1: 2, T2: 6.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>T1 can finish (needs 2, has 2 free). It returns its 4, so 4 are free.</li>\n<li>Now T0 needs 5 and T2 needs 6, but only 4 are free. Neither can be guaranteed to finish.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The state is <strong>unsafe</strong>. Unsafe does not mean deadlocked yet: it means a deadlock <em>can</em> happen if the threads ask for their maximums. This is exactly the state in 4.5's example after granting T2 one more unit, which is why the Banker's algorithm refuses that request.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s19","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s19","l":"S19","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s19\"><strong>S19.</strong> Classify each bug as an atomicity violation, an order violation or a deadlock, and give a fix: (a) Thread 1 checks <code>if (q-&gt;head != NULL)</code> and then reads <code>q-&gt;head-&gt;value</code>, while Thread 2 may remove the head. (b) The main thread creates a worker that immediately uses a global config pointer, and only afterwards does the main thread set that pointer. (c) Function <code>transfer(a, b)</code> locks account a, then account b; two threads call <code>transfer(x, y)</code> and <code>transfer(y, x)</code>.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a) <strong>Atomicity violation.</strong> The check and the use are meant to be one atomic step, but nothing makes them one. <strong>Fix:</strong> hold the queue's lock across both the check and the read, and in the remover.</p>\n<p>(b) <strong>Order violation.</strong> The worker assumes the pointer was set before it runs. <strong>Fix:</strong> set the pointer <em>before</em> creating the thread, or make the worker wait (on a semaphore initialized to 0, or a flag plus a CV) until main has set it.</p>\n<p>(c) <strong>Deadlock</strong> (circular wait): one thread holds x and wants y; the other holds y and wants x. <strong>Fix:</strong> always lock the two accounts in a global order, for example by account number or by address, whatever the argument order.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s20","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s20","l":"S20","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s20\"><strong>S20.</strong> A program sometimes hangs. <code>top -H</code> shows its threads at 0% CPU. What is the likely bug class, and how would you find exactly where each thread is stuck? What if they showed 100% CPU instead?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>0% CPU</strong> means the threads are asleep, blocked. The likely cause is a <strong>deadlock</strong>, or a <strong>lost wakeup</strong> (a thread sleeping in <code>cond_wait</code> that nobody will ever signal, like Tutorial 6's &quot;forgot to signal&quot; variant). To find it:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Attach with <code>gdb -p $(pgrep prog)</code>.</li>\n<li>Run <code>thread apply all bt</code>, which shows exactly which <code>pthread_mutex_lock</code> or <code>pthread_cond_wait</code> call, on which line, each thread is stuck in.</li>\n<li>Helgrind can also report lock-order inversions directly.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>100% CPU with no progress</strong> suggests a <strong>livelock</strong>, or threads spinning on a lock or flag. Use <code>perf</code> or gdb backtraces to see where they are looping.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s21","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s21","l":"S21","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s21\"><strong>S21 (theory).</strong> State the three requirements for a solution to the critical-section problem. For each of these, say which requirements it meets: (a) the load/store flag lock, (b) the test-and-set spin lock, (c) the ticket lock, (d) disabling interrupts on one CPU.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> The requirements are <strong>mutual exclusion, progress and bounded waiting</strong>. (a) The flag lock fails <strong>mutual exclusion</strong>: two threads can both see 0. (b) Test-and-set gives mutual exclusion and progress but <strong>not bounded waiting</strong>: a thread can lose every race. (c) The ticket lock meets <strong>all three</strong>, because tickets are served in FIFO order. (d) Disabling interrupts meets all three on a single CPU, but only if the code can be trusted not to keep interrupts off forever, and it does not work on several CPUs.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s22","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s22","l":"S22","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s22\"><strong>S22 (theory).</strong> Write Peterson's algorithm for two threads. Prove that it gives mutual exclusion and bounded waiting. Why does it fail on a modern multicore CPU without extra help?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> See the Peterson section above for the code: <code>flag[self] = 1; turn = other; while (flag[other] &amp;&amp; turn == other) ;</code>. <strong>Mutual exclusion:</strong> suppose both are inside. Then both flags are 1, so T0 could only pass if turn = 0, and T1 only if turn = 1. turn holds one value, so this is impossible: whichever thread wrote turn last sees <code>turn == other</code> and spins. <strong>Bounded waiting:</strong> if T0 waits, then when T1 leaves and tries again, T1 sets turn = 0 and so lets T0 in first. T0 waits for at most one entry by T1. <strong>Modern hardware:</strong> CPUs and compilers may reorder the stores to flag and turn with the loads after them, so both threads can read the other's flag as 0. It needs memory barriers.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s23","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s23","l":"S23","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s23\"><strong>S23 (theory).</strong> A program spends 20% of its time in serial code. (a) What is the maximum speedup on 4 cores, and on 16? (b) What is the limit as the cores grow without bound? (c) Explain the difference between concurrency and parallelism.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) On 4 cores: $1 / (0.2 + 0.8/4) = 1 / 0.4 = $ <strong>2.5</strong>. On 16 cores: $1 / (0.2 + 0.05) = $ <strong>4</strong>. (b) $1/0.2 = $ <strong>5</strong>, no matter how many cores. (c) <strong>Concurrency:</strong> several tasks are in progress at once, possibly by interleaving on one core. <strong>Parallelism:</strong> tasks run at the same instant on different cores. Parallelism needs several cores; concurrency does not.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s24","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s24","l":"S24","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s24\"><strong>S24 (theory).</strong> Compare user-level and kernel-level threads, and the many-to-one, one-to-one and many-to-many models. Which model does Linux use, and why?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> User-level threads are fast to create and switch (no system call), but one blocking system call blocks every thread in the process, and they cannot use several cores. Kernel threads can run in parallel and block independently, but each operation costs a system call. <strong>Many-to-one:</strong> no parallelism, and one block stops everything. <strong>One-to-one:</strong> true parallelism, at the cost of a kernel thread per user thread. <strong>Many-to-many:</strong> flexible, but complex to implement. Linux (and Windows) use <strong>one-to-one</strong>, because kernel threads are cheap enough today and the model is simple.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s25","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s25","l":"S25","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s25\"><strong>S25 (theory).</strong> An approximate counter has 4 CPUs and threshold S = 100. (a) Describe update and get. (b) How far can the global count lag behind the true count? (c) What happens if S = 1, and if S = 10⁶?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) <strong>Update:</strong> lock the local (per-CPU) counter and increment it. If it reaches S, lock the global counter, add the local value to it, and reset the local one to 0. <strong>Get:</strong> lock the global counter and return it (an approximation). (b) Each CPU can hold up to S − 1 unflushed updates, so the lag is up to about <strong>4 × 99 = 396</strong>. (c) With S = 1, every update touches the global lock, so it is exact but does not scale, like one lock. With S = 10⁶, it scales almost perfectly, but the global value can be wrong by millions.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s26","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s26","l":"S26","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s26\"><strong>S26 (theory).</strong> Explain Hoare and Mesa semantics for <code>signal</code>. Show a case where <code>if</code> is safe under Hoare semantics but unsafe under Mesa semantics. Which do real systems use?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Hoare:</strong> the signaller hands the lock and the CPU straight to the woken thread, so the condition is still true when that thread runs. <strong>Mesa:</strong> the woken thread only becomes ready, and runs later. Example: a consumer waits because count == 0. A producer puts an item and signals. Under Hoare, the consumer runs at once and the item is still there, so <code>if</code> is enough. Under Mesa, a second consumer can run first and take the item, so the woken consumer must re-check (<code>while</code>). Real systems (pthreads, Java, Linux) use <strong>Mesa</strong>, and also allow spurious wake-ups.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s27","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s27","l":"S27","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s27\"><strong>S27 (theory).</strong> Draw the resource-allocation graph for: T1 holds R1 and requests R2; T2 holds R2 and requests R3; T3 holds R3. (a) Is there a deadlock? (b) T3 now requests R1. Now? (c) If R2 had two instances, would a cycle always mean deadlock?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> The edges are R1 → T1, T1 → R2, R2 → T2, T2 → R3, R3 → T3. (a) <strong>No cycle, so no deadlock.</strong> T3 can finish and release R3, then T2, then T1. (b) Adding T3 → R1 creates the cycle T1 → R2 → T2 → R3 → T3 → R1 → T1. With one instance of each resource, a cycle <strong>is</strong> a deadlock. (c) <strong>No.</strong> With several instances, a cycle is necessary but not sufficient: another holder of R2 outside the cycle may finish and free an instance, which breaks the wait.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s28","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s28","l":"S28","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s28\"><strong>S28 (theory).</strong> How does a kernel implement a blocking semaphore? Why does it still need a spinlock or disabled interrupts, and why is that acceptable? How can the waiting list cause starvation?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> The semaphore holds a value and a <strong>list of waiting processes</strong>. <code>wait</code>: decrement; if the value is negative, add the caller to the list and block it (taking it off the run queue). <code>signal</code>: increment; if the value is ≤ 0, remove a process from the list and wake it (make it ready). The updates to the value and the list must be atomic, so the kernel disables interrupts (one CPU) or takes a spinlock (many CPUs). This is acceptable because the critical sections are only a few instructions long, unlike the application's critical section, which may be long. If the list is <strong>LIFO</strong>, a waiter can be overtaken forever (starvation). A <strong>FIFO</strong> list gives bounded waiting.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f1","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f1","l":"F1","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f1\"><strong>F1.</strong> A process runs the code below on a 300-byte file. What does each call return, and what is the offset after each?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">int fd = open(&quot;file&quot;, O_RDONLY);\nread(fd, buf, 120);\nlseek(fd, -50, SEEK_END);\nread(fd, buf, 100);\nread(fd, buf, 100);\n</code></pre>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <code>open</code> returns 3 (0-2 are taken), offset 0. <code>read</code> returns 120, offset 120. <code>lseek</code> sets the offset to 300 − 50 = <strong>250</strong> and returns 250. The next <code>read</code> asks for 100 but only 50 remain: returns <strong>50</strong>, offset 300. The last <code>read</code> returns <strong>0</strong> (end of file), offset stays 300.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f2","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f2","l":"F2","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f2\"><strong>F2.</strong> A parent opens a file, then forks. The child reads 100 bytes, then exits. The parent then reads 100 bytes. Which bytes does the parent get? What changes if the child instead opens the file itself after the fork?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> After <code>fork()</code> both fds point to the <strong>same open-file-table entry</strong>, which holds one offset. The child's read moves it to 100, so the parent reads <strong>bytes 100-199</strong>. If the child calls <code>open()</code> itself, it gets a <strong>new table entry</strong> with its own offset, and the parent reads <strong>bytes 0-99</strong>.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f3","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f3","l":"F3","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f3\"><strong>F3.</strong> You run <code>echo hi &gt; a; ln a b; ln -s a c; rm a</code>. What do <code>cat b</code> and <code>cat c</code> print, and what are the link counts?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <code>ln a b</code> makes <code>b</code> a second name for a's inode (link count 2). <code>ln -s a c</code> makes a new inode whose data is the path <code>&quot;a&quot;</code>; it does not change a's count. <code>rm a</code> unlinks the name <code>a</code>: count drops to <strong>1</strong>. <code>cat b</code> prints <strong>hi</strong> (the inode is still reachable). <code>cat c</code> follows the path <code>a</code>, which no longer exists: <strong>&quot;No such file or directory&quot;</strong> (a dangling link).</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f4","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f4","l":"F4","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f4\"><strong>F4.</strong> Why are hard links to directories not allowed, and why can't a hard link cross file systems?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> A hard link to a directory could create a <strong>cycle</strong> in the tree (a directory containing a link to its own ancestor), which breaks tools that walk the tree and makes <code>..</code> ambiguous. Inode numbers are only unique <strong>within one file system</strong>, so a directory entry on one FS cannot name an inode on another. Symbolic links store a path, so they have neither problem.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f5","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f5","l":"F5","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f5\"><strong>F5.</strong> An editor wants a crash during save never to leave a half-written file. Give the sequence of calls and say why each is needed.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> Write the new contents to <code>foo.txt.tmp</code>, <strong><code>fsync()</code></strong> it so the data really is on disk, <code>close()</code> it, then <strong><code>rename(&quot;foo.txt.tmp&quot;, &quot;foo.txt&quot;)</code></strong>. <code>rename</code> is atomic, so a crash leaves either the full old file or the full new one. Without the <code>fsync</code>, the rename could reach the disk before the data, and a crash would leave <code>foo.txt</code> pointing to empty or garbage blocks. For full safety, also <code>fsync</code> the directory after the rename.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f6","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f6","l":"F6","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f6\"><strong>F6. (slides)</strong> vsfs: 4 KB blocks, 256-byte inodes, inode table starting at 12 KB, 512-byte sectors. Find the inode block, the sector and the offset inside the block for inode 70. Then do inode 45.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> Inode 70: 70 × 256 = 17920 B; 17920 ÷ 4096 = iblock <strong>4</strong>; address 12 + 4 × 4 = <strong>28 KB</strong>; sector 28672 ÷ 512 = <strong>56</strong>; offset (70 mod 16) × 256 = <strong>1536 B</strong>. Inode 45: 45 × 256 = 11520 B; iblock <strong>2</strong>; address 12 + 8 = <strong>20 KB</strong>; sector <strong>40</strong>; offset (45 mod 16) × 256 = 13 × 256 = <strong>3328 B</strong>.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f7","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f7","l":"F7","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f7\"><strong>F7.</strong> A file system has 4 KB blocks, 4-byte block pointers, 12 direct pointers, and one single, one double and one triple indirect pointer. (a) What is the maximum file size? (b) Which pointer holds byte offset 100,000? (c) Byte offset 10,000,000?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">12</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1024</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8974em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">102</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1.0641em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">102</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">3</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\"> KB</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span></span></span></span> <strong>4 TB</strong> (just over <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">42</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> bytes). (b) 100,000 ÷ 4096 = block <strong>24</strong>. 24 ≥ 12, so it is in the <strong>single indirect</strong> block at slot 24 − 12 = <strong>12</strong>. (c) 10,000,000 ÷ 4096 = block 2441. 2441 − 12 = 2429 ≥ 1024, so subtract again: 2429 − 1024 = 1405. That is in the <strong>double indirect</strong> tree: outer slot 1405 ÷ 1024 = <strong>1</strong>, inner slot 1405 mod 1024 = <strong>381</strong>.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f8","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f8","l":"F8","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f8\"><strong>F8.</strong> Redo F7(a) for 1 KB blocks with the same pointers. Then compute xv6's maximum file size.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> A 1 KB block holds 1024 / 4 = 256 pointers: <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">12</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">256</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8974em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">25</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">6</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1.0641em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">25</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">6</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">3</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> blocks × 1 KB ≈ <strong>16 GB</strong> (16,843,020 KB). xv6 has only 12 direct pointers and one single indirect: (12 + 256) × 1 KB = <strong>268 KB</strong>.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f9","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f9","l":"F9","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f9\"><strong>F9.</strong> How many disk reads does <code>open(&quot;/usr/lib/libc.so&quot;)</code> need with a cold cache, assuming each directory fits in one block?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> Depth 3: root inode, root data, <code>usr</code> inode, <code>usr</code> data, <code>lib</code> inode, <code>lib</code> data, <code>libc.so</code> inode = <strong>7 reads</strong> = 2 × 3 + 1. The superblock is already in memory after mount.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f10","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f10","l":"F10","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f10\"><strong>F10. (slides)</strong> How many I/Os does it take to create <code>/foo/bar</code> and write 12 KB to it in vsfs (4 KB blocks, cold cache)? Which are user data?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> Create = <strong>10</strong>: read root inode, read root data, read foo inode, read foo data, read + write the inode bitmap, write foo's data (new entry), read + write bar's inode, write foo's inode. Each of the 3 allocating writes = <strong>5</strong>: read bar's inode, read + write the data bitmap, write the data block, write bar's inode. Total = 10 + 15 = <strong>25</strong>, and <strong>only 3</strong> of them are user data.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f11","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f11","l":"F11","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f11\"><strong>F11.</strong> Why does <code>read()</code> write the inode, and why does it never touch a bitmap? How could you avoid that write?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> It updates the inode's <strong>access time</strong> (<code>atime</code>). It touches no bitmap because reading never allocates or frees anything. Mount options like <code>noatime</code> or <code>relatime</code> skip or reduce the atime write.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f12","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f12","l":"F12","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f12\"><strong>F12. (slides)</strong> In <code>vsfs.py</code> the state is <code>inodes [d a:0 r:2] [] [] []</code>, <code>data [(.,0) (..,0)] [] [] []</code>. Show the state after <code>mkdir(&quot;/g&quot;)</code>, then <code>creat(&quot;/g/q&quot;)</code>, then <code>link(&quot;/g/q&quot;, &quot;/w&quot;)</code>.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <code>mkdir(&quot;/g&quot;)</code>: inodes <code>[d a:0 r:3] [d a:1 r:2] [] []</code> (root's count +1 for g's <code>..</code>), data <code>[(.,0) (..,0) (g,1)] [(.,1) (..,0)] [] []</code>. <code>creat(&quot;/g/q&quot;)</code>: inode 2 = <code>[f a:-1 r:1]</code> (no data block yet), g's block gets <code>(q,2)</code>. <code>link</code>: root's block gets <code>(w,2)</code> and inode 2 becomes <code>[f a:-1 r:2]</code>. No new inode or data block.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f13","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f13","l":"F13","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f13\"><strong>F13.</strong> Compare pointer-based allocation, extents and FAT on (a) metadata for a 1 GB contiguous file, (b) random access, (c) hard links.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) Pointers need one entry per block: 262,144 pointers plus indirect blocks. <strong>Extents need one</strong> (start, length). FAT needs one table entry per block. (b) Pointers: at most a few indirect reads. Extents: a short search over the extent list. FAT: <strong>walk the chain</strong> (fast only because the table is in memory). (c) Pointers and extents keep metadata in an inode, so hard links work. <strong>FAT has no inodes</strong>, so hard links are impossible.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f14","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f14","l":"F14","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f14\"><strong>F14.</strong> Give one example each of batching, scheduling and avoidance by write buffering, and say what an application that cannot lose data should do.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> Batching: two files created in the same inode-bitmap block → one bitmap write instead of two. Scheduling: 100 scattered dirty blocks written in disk order instead of arrival order. Avoidance: a temp file created and deleted within 30 s → no I/O at all. An application that cannot lose data calls <strong><code>fsync()</code></strong> at commit points (or uses <code>O_DIRECT</code>).</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f15","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f15","l":"F15","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f15\"><strong>F15.</strong> In xv6, why does <code>filewrite</code> split a large write into several <code>begin_op</code>/<code>end_op</code> transactions? Why does <code>writei</code> call <code>iupdate</code> even when the size did not change?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> A transaction must fit in the log: it may write at most <code>MAXOPBLOCKS</code> (10) blocks. Each written data block can also dirty a bitmap block, plus the inode, the indirect block and slop for unaligned ends, so <code>filewrite</code> limits each chunk to 3 data blocks. <code>writei</code> calls <code>iupdate</code> because <code>bmap</code> may have <strong>allocated a new block</strong> and stored it in <code>ip-&gt;addrs[]</code>, and that change must reach the on-disk inode.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f16","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f16","l":"F16","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f16\"><strong>F16 (theory).</strong> Compare contiguous, linked (including FAT) and indexed allocation on sequential access, random access, fragmentation and file growth. Which one is the UNIX inode, and why?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Contiguous:</strong> sequential and random access are both fast (block i is at start + i), but it causes <strong>external fragmentation</strong> and files are hard to grow. <strong>Linked:</strong> no external fragmentation and growth is easy, but random access must walk the chain (slow), and each block loses space to a pointer. <strong>FAT</strong> keeps the chain in a table in memory, which makes random access fast, but there are no inodes, so no hard links. <strong>Indexed:</strong> fast random access with no external fragmentation, but small files waste an index block, and large files need several levels. The UNIX inode is a <strong>combined indexed scheme</strong>: direct pointers for the many small files, and single, double and triple indirect blocks for the few large ones.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f17","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f17","l":"F17","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f17\"><strong>F17 (theory).</strong> A disk has 2 TB and 4 KB blocks. (a) How big is a free-space bitmap? (b) Compare the bitmap with a free list, grouping and counting.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) 2 TB / 4 KB = <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1.0641em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">41</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">12</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">29</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> blocks, so <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">29</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> bits = <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">26</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> bytes = <strong>64 MB</strong>. (b) The <strong>bitmap</strong> makes runs of free blocks easy to find, but must be cached in memory to be fast. A <strong>free list</strong> costs no extra space, but finding n free blocks takes n disk reads. <strong>Grouping</strong> stores n free addresses per block, so one read finds many. <strong>Counting</strong> stores (start, length) runs, which is very compact when free space comes in contiguous runs.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f18","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f18","l":"F18","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f18\"><strong>F18 (theory).</strong> Describe single-level, two-level, tree, acyclic-graph and general-graph directories. Why does UNIX forbid hard links to directories?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Single-level:</strong> one directory for everyone, so names clash. <strong>Two-level:</strong> one directory per user, which makes sharing hard. <strong>Tree:</strong> arbitrary depth, with absolute and relative paths, but no sharing. <strong>Acyclic graph:</strong> files can appear in several directories through links, so deletion needs reference counts (hard links) or leaves dangling links (soft links). <strong>General graph:</strong> cycles are allowed, so traversal can loop, and a file can be unreachable but still counted, which needs garbage collection. UNIX forbids hard links to directories so the directory structure stays <strong>acyclic</strong>: <code>..</code> stays well defined, tools like <code>find</code> cannot loop, and link counts reach 0 correctly.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f19","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f19","l":"F19","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f19\"><strong>F19 (theory).</strong> An append writes a data block, the inode and the data bitmap. For each case where exactly one or exactly two of these writes reach disk before a crash, say whether the file system is consistent and what the user sees.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Data only:</strong> consistent; the append is simply lost. <strong>Inode only:</strong> inconsistent (the inode points to a block the bitmap says is free), and the file reads garbage. <strong>Bitmap only:</strong> inconsistent, with a <strong>space leak</strong> (the block is used but nothing points to it). <strong>Inode and bitmap:</strong> metadata consistent, but the file contains <strong>garbage</strong>. <strong>Inode and data:</strong> inconsistent with the bitmap (the block could later be given to another file). <strong>Bitmap and data:</strong> space leak, and the data is unreachable.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f20","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f20","l":"F20","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f20\"><strong>F20 (theory).</strong> Explain how journaling recovers from a crash. What is the commit point? How does metadata (ordered) journaling differ from data journaling, and why must the data block be written first?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (1) <strong>Journal write:</strong> write TxBegin plus the new inode, bitmap (and data) blocks to the log. (2) <strong>Commit:</strong> write TxEnd. Once TxEnd is on disk, the transaction is <strong>committed</strong>: this is the commit point. (3) <strong>Checkpoint:</strong> write the blocks to their real locations. (4) <strong>Free</strong> the journal space. On recovery, replay every committed transaction and discard incomplete ones, so each update happens fully or not at all. <strong>Data journaling</strong> logs the user data too, so data is written twice. <strong>Metadata journaling</strong> logs only the inode and bitmap, and writes the data block to its final location <strong>before</strong> the commit. Otherwise a committed inode could point to a block still holding old garbage. Compared with fsck, recovery only reads the journal, not the whole disk.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f21","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f21","l":"F21","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f21\"><strong>F21 (theory).</strong> Give the three access methods, with one example where each is natural, and show how direct access is done with UNIX system calls.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Sequential:</strong> read the next record in order, for example an editor loading a file or a compiler reading source. <strong>Direct (random):</strong> jump to block or record n, for example a database looking up a row. <strong>Indexed:</strong> look up a key in an index, then access the block directly, for example a sorted product file. In UNIX, direct access is <code>lseek(fd, n * recsize, SEEK_SET)</code> followed by <code>read(fd, buf, recsize)</code>.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e1","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e1","l":"E1","s":"sheet Q1","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e1\"><strong>E1 (sheet Q1).</strong> A process has a 16 KB address space, loaded with base = 32 KB and bounds = 16 KB (bounds holds the size). (a) Translate 0, 1024, 3000, 4400, 16384 and 18000, or say which fault. [5] (b) Who sets base and bounds, when, and why must those instructions be privileged? [3] (c) Why does base and bounds waste memory? [2]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) A VA is legal only if VA &lt; bounds = 16384. Then PA = VA + 32768.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>VA</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>32768</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1024</td>\n<td>33792</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3000</td>\n<td>35768</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4400</td>\n<td><strong>37168</strong> (this faults in OSTEP's 4 KB version, but is valid here)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16384</td>\n<td><strong>Fault</strong>: VA = bounds, and the last legal address is 16383</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>18000</td>\n<td><strong>Fault</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(b) The OS sets them in kernel mode. It does this when it creates the process and on every context switch, restoring the values from the process's PCB. If a user program could change them, it could point base at another process's memory or at the kernel, so the instructions must be privileged.</p>\n<p>(c) The whole address space is allocated in physical memory, including the unused gap between heap and stack. That waste is <strong>internal fragmentation</strong>.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e2","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e2","l":"E2","s":"sheet Q2","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e2\"><strong>E2 (sheet Q2).</strong> The OS moves a descheduled process's address space to another region of physical memory. List the steps, and explain why the process never notices. [10]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>The process is already descheduled, so it is not running.</li>\n<li>The OS copies the address space from the old region to the new one.</li>\n<li>The OS writes the new base into the saved base register in the process's PCB.</li>\n<li>The OS adds the old region to the free list.</li>\n<li>On the next context switch to this process, the new base is loaded into the MMU.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The process only ever uses <strong>virtual</strong> addresses. Its VAs and bounds are unchanged, and only the hidden VA-to-PA mapping moved. This is dynamic relocation.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e3","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e3","l":"E3","s":"sheet Q3","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e3\"><strong>E3 (sheet Q3).</strong> 16-bit VAs: the top 2 bits pick the segment and the low 14 bits are the offset (max segment size 16 KB). The stack grows downward.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Segment</th>\n<th>Bits</th>\n<th>Base</th>\n<th>Size</th>\n<th>Grows positive?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Code</td>\n<td>00</td>\n<td>32 KB</td>\n<td>2 KB</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heap</td>\n<td>01</td>\n<td>34 KB</td>\n<td>3 KB</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stack</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>28 KB</td>\n<td>2 KB</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>unused</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>–</td>\n<td>–</td>\n<td>–</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(a) Translate 0x0100, 0x4200, 0x4C00, 0xFC00, 0xF000 and 0x8000. [7] (b) How does the hardware compute a stack address? [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a)</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>VA</th>\n<th>Segment</th>\n<th>Offset</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0x0100</td>\n<td>code</td>\n<td>256</td>\n<td>32768 + 256 = <strong>33024</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0x4200</td>\n<td>heap</td>\n<td>0x200 = 512</td>\n<td>34816 + 512 = <strong>35328</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0x4C00</td>\n<td>heap</td>\n<td>0xC00 = 3072</td>\n<td><strong>Fault</strong>: offset must be &lt; 3072 (one byte past the end)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0xFC00</td>\n<td>stack</td>\n<td>0x3C00 = 15360</td>\n<td>15360 − 16384 = −1024, so PA = 28672 − 1024 = <strong>27648</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0xF000</td>\n<td>stack</td>\n<td>0x3000 = 12288</td>\n<td>−4096, more than 2 KB below base: <strong>fault</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0x8000</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>–</td>\n<td><strong>Fault</strong>: unused segment</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(b) For a stack address, the hardware takes the offset and subtracts the maximum segment size: negative offset = offset − 16 KB. It checks that |negative offset| ≤ segment size, and then PA = base + negative offset. A &quot;grows positive&quot; bit in the segment register tells the hardware which rule to use.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e4","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e4","l":"E4","s":"sheet Q4","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e4\"><strong>E4 (sheet Q4).</strong> (a) Explain external fragmentation with a small example, and why compaction is expensive. [6] (b) How can several processes share a code segment safely? [4]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) Segments have different sizes, so allocating and freeing them leaves holes of different sizes between them. Example: free holes of 4 KB and 2 KB cannot satisfy a 5 KB segment, even though 6 KB is free. <strong>Compaction</strong> stops the processes, copies their segments together into one block, and updates their segment registers. That copies a lot of memory and pauses everything, and a growing segment can make the work wasted.</p>\n<p>(b) Each segment has <strong>protection bits</strong>. The code segment is marked read-execute, so every process can map the same physical code segment, and none of them can write to it. The hardware checks the bits on each access.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e5","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e5","l":"E5","s":"sheet Q5","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e5\"><strong>E5 (sheet Q5).</strong> The free list holds chunks of 10, 30, 20 and 15 bytes, in that order. Requests arrive for 12, 18 and 9 bytes. Ignore headers; a chunk is split and the remainder stays in place. (a) Show the list after each request under first fit, best fit and worst fit. [7] (b) A request for 25 then arrives. Which policies succeed, and what does that suggest? [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a)</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Policy</th>\n<th>After 12</th>\n<th>After 18</th>\n<th>After 9</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>First fit</td>\n<td>10, <strong>18</strong>, 20, 15</td>\n<td>10, 20, 15 (the 18 is used up)</td>\n<td><strong>1</strong>, 20, 15</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Best fit</td>\n<td>10, 30, 20, <strong>3</strong></td>\n<td>10, 30, <strong>2</strong>, 3</td>\n<td><strong>1</strong>, 30, 2, 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Worst fit</td>\n<td>10, <strong>18</strong>, 20, 15</td>\n<td>10, 18, <strong>2</strong>, 15</td>\n<td>10, <strong>9</strong>, 2, 15</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(b) Only <strong>best fit</strong> succeeds, using the 30. No policy is best for every workload. Here best fit kept a large chunk, but it also left tiny useless splinters (1, 2, 3).</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e6","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e6","l":"E6","s":"sheet Q6","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e6\"><strong>E6 (sheet Q6).</strong> A binary buddy allocator manages a 64 KB heap starting at 0. Requests arrive in order: A = 7 KB, B = 3 KB, C = 20 KB. (a) Which block does each get, and which splits are needed? [5] (b) What is the total internal fragmentation? [2] (c) A is freed, then B. Describe the coalescing. [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) Round each request up to a power of two.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A needs 8 KB. Split 64 into 32 + 32, then [0, 32) into 16 + 16, then [0, 16) into 8 + 8. <strong>A = [0, 8 KB)</strong>.</li>\n<li>B needs 4 KB. Split [8, 16) into 4 + 4. <strong>B = [8, 12 KB)</strong>.</li>\n<li>C needs 32 KB. <strong>C = [32, 64 KB)</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(b) (8 − 7) + (4 − 3) + (32 − 20) = <strong>14 KB</strong>.</p>\n<p>(c) When A is freed, its buddy [8, 16) is split and part of it holds B, so nothing merges. When B is freed, it merges with [12, 16) into [8, 16). That merges with [0, 8) into [0, 16), and that merges with [16, 32) into [0, 32). It stops there, because the buddy [32, 64) holds C.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e7","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e7","l":"E7","s":"sheet Q7","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e7\"><strong>E7 (sheet Q7).</strong> 32-bit VAs, 4 KB pages, 4-byte PTEs and a linear page table. (a) VPN and offset bits? [2] (b) Table size per process, and for 100 processes? [3] (c) VA 0x00403A2C maps to a page whose PTE holds PFN 0x01F2. What is the PA? [3] (d) Without a TLB, how many memory references does one load need? [2]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) 12-bit offset (2^12 = 4 KB) and a 20-bit VPN. (b) 2^20 × 4 B = <strong>4 MB</strong> per process, <strong>400 MB</strong> for 100 processes. (c) VPN = 0x00403 and offset = 0xA2C, so PA = <strong>0x01F2A2C</strong>: the PFN followed by the unchanged offset. (d) <strong>2</strong>: one to read the PTE and one for the data.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e8","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e8","l":"E8","s":"sheet Q8","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e8\"><strong>E8 (sheet Q8).</strong> What happens, and who handles it? (a) A load to a page with valid = 0. (b) valid = 1, present = 0. (c) A store to a read-only page. (d) How are the dirty and reference bits used in eviction? [10]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) The hardware raises an exception and the OS handles it. The address is not part of the address space, so the OS usually kills the process (segmentation fault).</li>\n<li>(b) <strong>Page fault.</strong> The OS page-fault handler finds the swap address stored in the PTE and reads the page from disk; the process is blocked during the I/O. It evicts a page first if memory is full. It then sets present = 1 and the PFN, and retries the instruction.</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>Protection fault</strong>: a trap to the OS, which usually kills the process. With copy-on-write (as in MP2), the OS instead copies the page, makes it writable and retries the instruction.</li>\n<li>(d) A clean page can be dropped for free, but a dirty one must be written back to disk first, so the OS prefers clean pages. The reference (use) bit is set on each access, and the clock algorithm uses it to approximate LRU: it skips pages with use = 1, clearing the bit as it passes.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e9","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e9","l":"E9","s":"sheet Q9","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e9\"><strong>E9 (sheet Q9).</strong> An array of ten 4-byte ints a[0..9] starts at VA 100. Pages are 16 bytes, and the TLB starts empty. (a) Which pages hold the array? Mark each access as a hit or miss, and give the hit rate. [5] (b) The loop runs again right away. What is the hit rate, and which locality explains it? [2] (c) What would the first-loop hit rate be with 4 KB pages? [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) The array covers bytes 100 to 139. VPN 6 (96–111) holds a[0..2], VPN 7 (112–127) holds a[3..6], and VPN 8 (128–143) holds a[7..9]. The pattern is miss, hit, hit, miss, hit, hit, hit, miss, hit, hit, so the hit rate is <strong>70%</strong>. Spatial locality.</p>\n<p>(b) <strong>100%</strong> (assuming the TLB still holds the three entries). Temporal locality.</p>\n<p>(c) Bytes 100 to 139 all fall in page 0, so there is 1 miss and the hit rate is <strong>90%</strong>. Bigger pages capture more spatial locality.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e10","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e10","l":"E10","s":"sheet Q10","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e10\"><strong>E10 (sheet Q10).</strong> A TLB lookup takes 1 ns and a memory access 100 ns. On a miss, the hardware reads one PTE from memory. (a) Find the effective access time for hit rates of 98% and 90%. [5] (b) P1 and P2 both use VPN 10, mapped to different frames. What problem does this cause on a context switch? Give two fixes and their trade-offs. [5]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) A hit costs 1 + 100 = 101 ns. A miss costs 1 + 100 (PTE) + 100 (data) = 201 ns.</p>\n<div class=\"math-d\" data-tex=\"\\text{EAT} = h(t_{TLB} + t_M) + (1-h)(t_{TLB} + 2t_M)\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">EAT</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">h</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">T</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">L</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0502em;\">B</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">M</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">h</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">T</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">L</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0502em;\">B</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">M</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li>At 98%: 0.98 × 101 + 0.02 × 201 = <strong>103 ns</strong>.</li>\n<li>At 90%: 90.9 + 20.1 = <strong>111 ns</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(b) After a switch, the TLB still holds P1's VPN 10 → PFN entry, so P2 would access P1's frame. <strong>Fix 1: flush the TLB</strong> on every switch. It is simple, but every switch then starts with a cold TLB and a burst of misses. <strong>Fix 2: an address-space identifier (ASID)</strong> in each entry, so entries from different processes can coexist. There is no flush, but it needs extra hardware bits and the OS must manage the ASIDs.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e11","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e11","l":"E11","s":"sheet Q11","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e11\"><strong>E11 (sheet Q11).</strong> A 16 KB address space (14-bit VAs), 64-byte pages and 4-byte PTEs. The process uses only VPNs 0, 1 (code), 4, 5 (heap), 254 and 255 (stack). (a) VPN and offset bits? How large would a linear table be? [2] (b) With one page of PTEs per directory entry, how many bits index the directory and how many index a page of the table? [2] (c) How many pages does the two-level table need? [2] (d) Split 0x3F80 into directory index, table index and offset. Its PTE holds PFN 55. What is the PA? [4]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) 6-bit offset, 8-bit VPN. A linear table has 256 × 4 B = <strong>1 KB = 16 pages</strong>. (b) A page holds 64 / 4 = 16 PTEs, so <strong>4 bits</strong> index a page of the table and <strong>4 bits</strong> index the directory. (c) The directory, plus the table page for VPNs 0–15, plus the table page for VPNs 240–255: <strong>3 pages</strong> instead of 16. (d) 0x3F80 = 11 1111 1000 0000. The VPN is 1111 1110 = 254: <strong>directory index 15, table index 14, offset 0</strong>. PA = 55 × 64 = <strong>3520 (0xDC0)</strong>.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e12","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e12","l":"E12","s":"sheet Q12","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e12\"><strong>E12 (sheet Q12).</strong> 48-bit VAs, 4 KB pages, 8-byte PTEs, and every level must fit in one page. (a) How many levels are needed? [5] (b) How many memory references does a load need on a TLB miss, and what is the trade-off? [5]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) One page holds 4096 / 8 = 512 PTEs, so each level uses 9 bits. The VPN is 48 − 12 = 36 bits, and 36 / 9 = <strong>4 levels</strong>. (b) <strong>5</strong>: one per level plus the data. Multi-level tables trade <strong>time for space</strong>. A TLB miss costs more memory references, but table pages exist only for the parts of the address space in use. They are also more complex to implement.</p>\n<p><em>Likely twist:</em> RISC-V Sv39 (Tutorial 3 and xv6) has 39-bit VAs, so 27 VPN bits and <strong>3 levels</strong> of 9 bits.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e13","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e13","l":"E13","s":"sheet Q13","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e13\"><strong>E13 (sheet Q13).</strong> <code>lock: while (m-&gt;flag == 1) ; m-&gt;flag = 1;</code> and <code>unlock: m-&gt;flag = 0;</code> (a) Give an interleaving with two threads in the critical section. [3] (b) Rewrite lock() with TestAndSet. [3] (c) Evaluate the spin lock for correctness, fairness and single-CPU performance. [4]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) flag = 0. T1 reads flag = 0 and leaves the loop, then a timer interrupt switches to T2. T2 reads 0, sets flag = 1 and enters. T1 resumes, sets flag = 1 and enters as well. Both are inside.</p>\n<p>(b)</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">void lock(lock_t *m) { while (TestAndSet(&amp;m-&gt;flag, 1) == 1) ; }  // spin\nvoid unlock(lock_t *m) { m-&gt;flag = 0; }\n</code></pre>\n<p>(c) <strong>Correct</strong>: yes, the test and the set happen as one atomic step. <strong>Fair</strong>: no, a thread can spin forever while others keep winning. <strong>Performance on one CPU</strong>: poor. If the holder is preempted, each waiter spins for its whole time slice, so N threads can waste N − 1 slices. On multiple CPUs it is reasonable when critical sections are short.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e14","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e14","l":"E14","s":"sheet Q14","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e14\"><strong>E14 (sheet Q14).</strong> A ticket lock: <code>myturn = FetchAndAdd(&amp;ticket); while (turn != myturn) ;</code> and <code>unlock: turn++</code>, with ticket = turn = 0. (a) T1, T2 and T3 call lock in that order. Give each myturn and the order they enter. [3] (b) T1 calls unlock twice by mistake. What happens? [4] (c) Why is this fair when test-and-set is not? [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) T1 gets 0, T2 gets 1 and T3 gets 2. They enter in the order T1, T2, T3.</p>\n<p>(b) The first unlock sets turn = 1, and T2 enters. The second sets turn = 2, and <strong>T3 enters while T2 is still inside</strong>. turn now stays one ahead of where it should be, so later threads keep overlapping in pairs. Mutual exclusion is broken from then on.</p>\n<p>(c) Each thread gets a ticket and is served in ticket order (FIFO), so every thread eventually gets in. With test-and-set, whoever happens to run when the lock is released wins, so a thread can starve.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e15","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e15","l":"E15","s":"sheet Q15","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e15\"><strong>E15 (sheet Q15).</strong> Two broken joins. Version 1 (no state variable): exit = lock, signal, unlock; join = lock, wait, unlock. Version 2 (no lock): exit = <code>done = 1; signal</code>; join = <code>if (done == 0) wait</code>. (a) and (b) Give an interleaving in which each version makes the parent wait forever. [3 + 3] (c) Write the correct version. [4]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) Version 1: the child runs first and calls thr_exit. Its signal finds no waiter and is lost. The parent then calls wait and sleeps forever.</p>\n<p>(b) Version 2: the parent checks done == 0 and is interrupted before it calls wait. The child sets done = 1 and signals, but nobody is waiting. The parent resumes, calls wait, and sleeps forever.</p>\n<p>(c)</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">void thr_exit() { lock(&amp;m); done = 1; signal(&amp;c); unlock(&amp;m); }\nvoid thr_join() { lock(&amp;m); while (done == 0) wait(&amp;c, &amp;m); unlock(&amp;m); }\n</code></pre>\n<p>Rules: always use a state variable, hold the lock around signal and wait, and check the condition in a while loop.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e16","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e16","l":"E16","s":"sheet Q16","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e16\"><strong>E16 (sheet Q16).</strong> allocate(n) waits on one CV while bytesLeft &lt; n. free(n) adds n and calls signal. bytesLeft starts at 0. (a) Ta calls allocate(100) and Tb calls allocate(10), and both wait. Tc calls free(50). Show how a thread can stay asleep even though memory is free. [4] (b) Give the fix and its cost. [3] (c) Why must allocate use a while loop? [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) free(50) sets bytesLeft = 50, and signal wakes one waiter. If it wakes Ta, Ta rechecks 50 &lt; 100 and sleeps again. Tb, which needs only 10, is never woken.</p>\n<p>(b) Use <strong>broadcast</strong>, which wakes all waiters. This is a <strong>covering condition</strong>. The cost is that threads that still cannot proceed also wake up, recheck and sleep again, which wastes context switches.</p>\n<p>(c) Under Mesa semantics, a woken thread does not run at once. Another thread can take the memory first, and spurious wake-ups can happen, so the condition must be rechecked after every wake-up.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e17","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e17","l":"E17","s":"sheet Q17","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e17\"><strong>E17 (sheet Q17).</strong> Thread A prints A1 then A2. Thread B prints B1 then B2. (a) Using semaphores, ensure A1 comes before B2 and B1 comes before A2. Give initial values. [6] (b) What goes wrong if both threads call sem_wait before sem_post? [4]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) This is the <strong>rendezvous</strong>. Use two semaphores, <code>aArrived</code> and <code>bArrived</code>, both starting at 0.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">// Thread A                 // Thread B\nprint(&quot;A1&quot;);                print(&quot;B1&quot;);\nsem_post(&amp;aArrived);        sem_post(&amp;bArrived);\nsem_wait(&amp;bArrived);        sem_wait(&amp;aArrived);\nprint(&quot;A2&quot;);                print(&quot;B2&quot;);\n</code></pre>\n<p>(b) <strong>Deadlock.</strong> Each thread waits for a post that the other only makes after its own wait, so both sleep forever.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e18","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e18","l":"E18","s":"sheet Q18","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e18\"><strong>E18 (sheet Q18).</strong> The semaphore reader-writer lock, where the first reader waits on writelock and the last reader posts it. (a) What are the initial values of lock and writelock? Why do only the first and last readers touch writelock? [4] (b) Give a scenario in which a writer waits forever. [3] (c) Suggest a fix. [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) Both start at <strong>1</strong>. <code>lock</code> protects the readers count. The first reader takes writelock for the whole group of readers, which blocks writers. Middle readers only update the count, and the last reader to leave releases writelock.</p>\n<p>(b) R1 holds the read lock, and W waits on writelock. R2 arrives before R1 leaves, then R3 before R2 leaves, and so on. readers never drops to 0, so W waits forever.</p>\n<p>(c) Writer priority: add a semaphore <code>turnstile</code> (initial 1). A writer does sem_wait(turnstile) before waiting on writelock and posts it after. Every reader does sem_wait(turnstile) and sem_post(turnstile) on entry, so new readers queue up behind a waiting writer. (This is Homework 04.)</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e19","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e19","l":"E19","s":"sheet Q19","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e19\"><strong>E19 (sheet Q19).</strong> VSFS: 4 KB blocks and 256-byte inodes. Block 0 is the superblock, block 1 the inode bitmap, block 2 the data bitmap, and the inode table starts at block 3. (a) In which block, and at what byte address, is inode 37? [4] (b) An inode has 12 direct pointers, 1 single-indirect and 1 double-indirect pointer, and pointers are 4 bytes. What is the maximum file size? [4] (c) Why is this design a good fit for real files? [2]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) Inode 37 starts 37 × 256 = 9472 bytes into the table. 9472 / 4096 gives inode-table block 2, so it is in <strong>disk block 5</strong>. Byte address = 12288 + 9472 = <strong>21760</strong>, which is offset (37 mod 16) × 256 = 1280 into the block. Sector = 21760 / 512 = <strong>42</strong> (it starts halfway through that sector). The slide used inode 32, which sits at the start of a block; 37 does not.</p>\n<p>(b) A block holds 4096 / 4 = 1024 pointers.</p>\n<div class=\"math-d\" data-tex=\"(12 + 1024 + 1024^2) \\times 4\\text{ KB} = 48\\text{ KB} + 4\\text{ MB} + 4\\text{ GB} \\approx 4.004\\text{ GB}\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">12</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1024</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1.1141em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">102</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8641em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.113em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\"> KB</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">48</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\"> KB</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\"> MB</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\"> GB</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4.004</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\"> GB</span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n<p>That is 4,299,210,752 bytes.</p>\n<p>(c) Most files are small, so direct pointers serve them with no extra reads. The few large files pay for the indirection. The tree is deliberately unbalanced.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e20","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e20","l":"E20","s":"sheet Q20","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e20\"><strong>E20 (sheet Q20).</strong> Nothing is cached, and each directory fits in one block. List the disk I/Os for (a) <code>open(&quot;/foo/bar&quot;, O_RDONLY)</code> [3], (b) the first 4 KB <code>read()</code> [3], and (c) creating <code>/foo/bar</code> [4].</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) Read the root inode, root data, foo inode, foo data, then bar's inode: <strong>5 reads</strong> (2 × depth + 1).</p>\n<p>(b) Read bar's inode, read the data block, and write bar's inode to update its access time: <strong>2 reads and 1 write</strong>. No bitmap is touched.</p>\n<p>(c) Read the root inode, root data, foo inode and foo data. Read the inode bitmap and write it back. Write foo's data (the new entry). Read bar's inode and write it (it shares a block with other inodes, so this is a read-modify-write). Write foo's inode. That is <strong>10 I/Os: 6 reads and 4 writes</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Likely follow-up:</em> each allocating write takes 5 I/Os (read inode, read data bitmap, write data bitmap, write data, write inode). Creating a 12 KB file therefore takes 10 + 3 × 5 = <strong>25 I/Os</strong>.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras#p-x1","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras.html#p-x1","l":"X1","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: post-midsem extras","q":"<p id=\"p-x1\"><strong>X1.</strong> Hybrid paging and segmentation (L13 s8-11). 32-bit VAs and 4 KB pages. The top 2 bits pick the segment (01 code, 10 heap, 11 stack, 00 unused), then an 18-bit VPN, then a 12-bit offset. Each segment has a base register (the physical address of its page table) and a bounds register (the number of valid pages). Code bounds = 3, heap base = 0x2000 and heap bounds = 10, with 4-byte PTEs. (a) Where is the PTE for VA 0x80001234? [3] (b) What happens on VA 0x40003ABC? [2] (c) The process uses 3 code pages, 10 heap pages and 2 stack pages. Compare the page-table memory with a linear table. [3] (d) Give two drawbacks of the hybrid. [2]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) Top bits 10 = heap. VPN = (VA &amp; VPN_MASK) &gt;&gt; 12 = 1, and the offset is 0x234. 1 &lt; 10, so it is in bounds. AddressOfPTE = Base[heap] + VPN × 4 = 0x2000 + 4 = <strong>0x2004</strong>.</p>\n<p>(b) Top bits 01 = code, VPN = 3, offset 0xABC. Code bounds = 3, so valid VPNs are 0 to 2. 3 is out of bounds: an <strong>exception</strong>, and the process is likely killed.</p>\n<p>(c) A linear table needs 2^20 × 4 B = <strong>4 MB</strong>. The hybrid stores only 3 + 10 + 2 = 15 PTEs = <strong>60 B</strong>, because the unused pages between heap and stack cost no table space.</p>\n<p>(d) It still needs segmentation, which assumes a fixed pattern of address-space use (a large sparse heap still wastes table space). Page tables are now variable-sized, so <strong>external fragmentation</strong> comes back when allocating them.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras#p-x2","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras.html#p-x2","l":"X2","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: post-midsem extras","q":"<p id=\"p-x2\"><strong>X2.</strong> Inverted page tables, and swapping page tables (L13 s26-27). (a) What is an inverted page table, and how big is it for 4 GB of RAM, 4 KB frames and 8-byte entries? [4] (b) Why does lookup need a hash table? [3] (c) What does it mean to swap page tables to disk? [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) There is <strong>one table for the whole system</strong>, with one entry per <strong>physical frame</strong>, saying which process and VPN use that frame. 4 GB / 4 KB = 2^20 frames × 8 B = <strong>8 MB in total</strong>, however many processes run. Linear tables would need 4 MB <em>per process</em> on a 32-bit system.</p>\n<p>(b) The table is indexed by frame, but translation starts from (process, VPN). A linear search over 2^20 entries per TLB miss is too slow, so a hash on (PID, VPN) finds the entry.</p>\n<p>(c) The page tables themselves are placed in kernel virtual memory, so the OS can page parts of them out to disk under memory pressure. A TLB miss can then require a page fault just to bring in the page-table page.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras#p-x3","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras.html#p-x3","l":"X3","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: post-midsem extras","q":"<p id=\"p-x3\"><strong>X3.</strong> The swapping mechanism (L13 s33-43). (a) On a TLB miss, list the three cases the hardware or OS can find in the PTE, and what happens in each. [4] (b) Give the steps the OS takes to service a page fault when memory is full. [3] (c) What are the high and low watermarks, and why does the OS evict pages in clusters? [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a)</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Valid and present</strong>: insert the translation into the TLB and retry, which now hits.</li>\n<li><strong>Valid but not present</strong>: page fault. The OS brings the page in from swap, updates the PTE and retries.</li>\n<li><strong>Not valid</strong>: an illegal access, so a segmentation fault, and the OS kills the process.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>(b) Find a free frame. If there is none, run the replacement policy to choose a victim, write it back if it is dirty, and mark its PTE not present. Issue the disk read for the faulting page, blocking the process; another process runs meanwhile. When the I/O finishes, set present = 1 and the PFN in the PTE, and retry the instruction.</p>\n<p>(c) The OS does not wait until memory is completely full. When free pages fall below the <strong>low watermark (LW)</strong>, the <strong>swap daemon</strong> (page daemon) evicts pages until there are <strong>high watermark (HW)</strong> pages free, then sleeps. Writing many pages at once in a <strong>cluster</strong> lets the disk do one large sequential write instead of many seeks, which is far faster.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras#p-x4","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras.html#p-x4","l":"X4","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: post-midsem extras","q":"<p id=\"p-x4\"><strong>X4.</strong> Producer/consumer with condition variables (L16 s14-21). One producer and two consumers share a one-slot buffer.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">void *consumer() { lock(&amp;m); if (count == 0) wait(&amp;cv, &amp;m); get(); signal(&amp;cv); unlock(&amp;m); }\nvoid *producer() { lock(&amp;m); if (count == 1) wait(&amp;cv, &amp;m); put(); signal(&amp;cv); unlock(&amp;m); }\n</code></pre>\n<p>(a) Show how a consumer can call get() on an empty buffer. [4] (b) After changing <code>if</code> to <code>while</code>, show how all three threads can end up asleep. [4] (c) Give the final fix. [2]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) Tc1 finds the buffer empty and waits. P fills the buffer and signals, so Tc1 becomes ready but does not run yet. Tc2 runs first and consumes. Tc1 then runs, does not recheck because of the <code>if</code>, and calls get() on an empty buffer.</p>\n<p>(b) Tc1 and Tc2 both wait. P fills, signals (waking Tc1) and then waits because the buffer is full. Tc1 consumes and signals, but there is <strong>one CV</strong>, and the signal wakes Tc2 instead of P. Tc2 sees an empty buffer and waits. Now all three are asleep.</p>\n<p>(c) Use <code>while</code>, plus <strong>two CVs</strong>: producers wait on <code>empty</code> and signal <code>fill</code>, and consumers wait on <code>fill</code> and signal <code>empty</code>.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras#p-x5","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras.html#p-x5","l":"X5","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: post-midsem extras","q":"<p id=\"p-x5\"><strong>X5.</strong> Zemaphores and throttling (L17 s26-28). (a) Implement <code>Zem_wait</code> and <code>Zem_post</code> using one lock and one condition variable. [5] (b) How does a Zemaphore's value differ from a Dijkstra semaphore's? [2] (c) What is thread throttling, and what initial value does it use? [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a)</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">typedef struct { int value; pthread_cond_t cond; pthread_mutex_t lock; } Zem_t;\nvoid Zem_init(Zem_t *s, int value) { s-&gt;value = value; /* init cond and lock */ }\nvoid Zem_wait(Zem_t *s) {\n    lock(&amp;s-&gt;lock);\n    while (s-&gt;value &lt;= 0) wait(&amp;s-&gt;cond, &amp;s-&gt;lock);\n    s-&gt;value--;\n    unlock(&amp;s-&gt;lock);\n}\nvoid Zem_post(Zem_t *s) {\n    lock(&amp;s-&gt;lock);\n    s-&gt;value++;\n    signal(&amp;s-&gt;cond);\n    unlock(&amp;s-&gt;lock);\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>(b) In Dijkstra's version, sem_wait decrements first, so a negative value −k means k threads are waiting. A Zemaphore waits <em>before</em> decrementing, so its value <strong>never goes below 0</strong>. (This matches Linux.)</p>\n<p>(c) <strong>Admission control</strong>: a semaphore initialised to <strong>N</strong> limits how many threads can be inside a region at once, for example a memory-hungry phase, so the system does not thrash. Initial values to remember: lock 1, ordering 0, bounded buffer empty = MAX and full = 0, throttling N.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras#p-x6","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras.html#p-x6","l":"X6","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: post-midsem extras","q":"<p id=\"p-x6\"><strong>X6.</strong> File API details (L18 s16-25). (a) <code>ls -l</code> shows <code>-rwxr-x--- 2 alice staff</code>. Give the octal mode, and say what group and others can do. What is the 2? [4] (b) What do r, w and x mean on a <strong>directory</strong>? [2] (c) You run <code>mkdir d; mkdir d/e; mkdir d/f</code>. What is the link count of d, and why? [2] (d) What does <code>mount</code> do? [2]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) rwx = 7, r-x = 5, --- = 0, so the mode is <strong>750</strong>. The group can read and execute; others have no access. The 2 is the <strong>hard-link count</strong>: two directory entries name this inode.</p>\n<p>(b) r = list the names in it, w = create, delete or rename entries (needs x as well), x = enter it (cd) and access files inside it by name.</p>\n<p>(c) <strong>4</strong>: the entry <code>d</code> in its parent, <code>.</code> inside d, and <code>..</code> inside e and inside f. Every new subdirectory adds 1.</p>\n<p>(d) mount attaches a file system's root at a directory (the mount point) in the existing tree, so all file systems appear as one tree. For example, <code>mount -t ext3 /dev/sda1 /home/users</code>.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras#p-x7","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras.html#p-x7","l":"X7","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: post-midsem extras","q":"<p id=\"p-x7\"><strong>X7.</strong> Lock primitives (L15 s20-29). (a) Write CompareAndSwap and a spin lock built on it. [3] (b) Write a lock with LoadLinked and StoreConditional. [3] (c) With <code>yield</code> instead of spinning, N threads on one CPU contend and the holder is preempted. What is the cost, and what problem remains? [4]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a)</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">int CompareAndSwap(int *ptr, int expected, int new) {\n    int actual = *ptr;\n    if (actual == expected) *ptr = new;\n    return actual;            // all of this happens atomically\n}\nvoid lock(lock_t *l) { while (CompareAndSwap(&amp;l-&gt;flag, 0, 1) == 1) ; }\n</code></pre>\n<p>(b)</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">void lock(lock_t *l) {\n    while (1) {\n        while (LoadLinked(&amp;l-&gt;flag) == 1) ;               // spin until free\n        if (StoreConditional(&amp;l-&gt;flag, 1) == 1) return;   // fails if anyone wrote since LL\n    }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>(c) Each waiter runs, finds the lock held and yields at once, so a round costs up to <strong>N − 1 context switches</strong> instead of N − 1 whole time slices. That is better than spinning, but context switches are still expensive with many threads. Starvation also remains: a thread can keep yielding while others get the lock. Hence queues with <strong>park/unpark</strong>.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p1","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p1","l":"P1","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p1\"><strong>P1.</strong> (a) What does this print, and in what order? [4] (b) Define zombie and orphan. What does xv6's exit() do with a dying process's children? [3] (c) Why are fork and exec separate calls? [3]</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">int main() {\n    int x = 1;\n    if (fork() == 0) {\n        x++;\n        if (fork() == 0) { x++; printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, x); exit(0); }\n        wait(NULL); printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, x);\n    } else { wait(NULL); printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, x); }\n}\n</code></pre>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) It prints <strong>3, 2, 1</strong>, always in that order. The grandchild has x = 3 and prints first. The child (x = 2) waits for the grandchild, and the parent (x = 1) waits for the child. Each process has its own copy of x.</p>\n<p>(b) A <strong>zombie</strong> has exited, but its parent has not called wait() yet, so its process-table entry and exit status are kept. An <strong>orphan</strong> is a process whose parent exited first. xv6's exit() reparents the children to init, which waits on them, sets the process state to ZOMBIE, and wakes the parent.</p>\n<p>(c) Between fork and exec, the shell can change the child's environment, for example <code>close(1); open(&quot;out.txt&quot;, ...)</code> for redirection, or setting up pipes. The program it then runs inherits those changes without knowing about them.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p2","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p2","l":"P2","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p2\"><strong>P2.</strong> Limited direct execution. (a) List the hardware and OS steps when a timer interrupt switches from process A to process B. [6] (b) Which registers are saved twice, by whom, and where? [2] (c) Why does the trap table have to be set up at boot, and why is a system-call <em>number</em> used instead of an address? [2]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a)</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Hardware</strong>: the timer interrupt fires. A's user registers are saved to A's kernel stack, the CPU switches to kernel mode, and it jumps to the trap handler.</li>\n<li><strong>OS</strong>: the handler decides to switch and calls <code>switch()</code>, which saves A's kernel registers into A's proc structure.</li>\n<li><strong>OS</strong>: it restores B's kernel registers from B's proc structure and switches to B's kernel stack.</li>\n<li><strong>OS</strong>: return-from-trap.</li>\n<li><strong>Hardware</strong>: restores B's user registers from B's kernel stack, switches to user mode, and jumps to B's PC.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>(b) The <strong>user registers</strong> are saved by the hardware onto the kernel stack (in xv6, the trapframe). The <strong>kernel registers</strong> are saved by the OS in <code>swtch()</code> into the proc structure (<code>struct context</code>: ra, sp and s0–s11).</p>\n<p>(c) The trap table is installed in kernel mode at boot, using a privileged instruction, so user code cannot redirect traps. User code passes a number, and the kernel validates it and looks up the handler. That way user code can never jump to an arbitrary kernel address.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p3","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p3","l":"P3","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p3\"><strong>P3.</strong> Jobs: A arrives at 0 and needs 6; B arrives at 2 and needs 3; C at 4 needs 8; D at 6 needs 2. Find the average turnaround and response times for FIFO, SJF (non-preemptive), STCF and RR (q = 2; a new arrival joins the queue before the job that was just preempted). [10]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Policy</th>\n<th>Schedule</th>\n<th>Completion A, B, C, D</th>\n<th>Avg turnaround</th>\n<th>Avg response</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>FIFO</td>\n<td>A 0–6, B 6–9, C 9–17, D 17–19</td>\n<td>6, 9, 17, 19</td>\n<td>(6+7+13+13)/4 = <strong>9.75</strong></td>\n<td>(0+4+5+11)/4 = <strong>5</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SJF</td>\n<td>A 0–6, D 6–8, B 8–11, C 11–19</td>\n<td>6, 11, 19, 8</td>\n<td>(6+9+15+2)/4 = <strong>8</strong></td>\n<td>(0+6+7+0)/4 = <strong>3.25</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>STCF</td>\n<td>A 0–2, B 2–5, A 5–6, D 6–8, A 8–11, C 11–19</td>\n<td>11, 5, 19, 8</td>\n<td>(11+3+15+2)/4 = <strong>7.75</strong></td>\n<td>(0+0+7+0)/4 = <strong>1.75</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RR</td>\n<td>A 0–2, B 2–4, A 4–6, C 6–8, B 8–9, D 9–11, A 11–13, C 13–19</td>\n<td>13, 9, 19, 11</td>\n<td>(13+7+15+5)/4 = <strong>10</strong></td>\n<td>(0+0+2+3)/4 = <strong>1.25</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>STCF is best for turnaround and RR is best for response. Turnaround = completion − arrival, and response = first run − arrival.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p4","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p4","l":"P4","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p4\"><strong>P4.</strong> MLFQ with three queues: Q2 (top, quantum 10 ms), Q1 (20 ms) and Q0 (round robin), with no boost. A is CPU-bound and needs 100 ms, arriving at 0. B needs 20 ms and arrives at 30. (a) Trace the schedule and give both turnaround times. [4] (b) Show how a job can game Rules 4a/4b, and give the fix. [3] (c) Why is a priority boost needed, and what goes wrong if it is too frequent? [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) A runs 0–10 in Q2 and moves down to Q1. It runs 10–30 in Q1 and moves to Q0. B arrives at 30 in Q2 and runs 30–40, then moves to Q1. B runs 40–50 in Q1 and finishes. A resumes at 50 with 70 ms left and finishes at 120. <strong>Turnaround: B = 20 and A = 120.</strong> MLFQ approximates SJF without knowing job lengths.</p>\n<p>(b) Under the old rule, a job that gives up the CPU before its slice ends keeps its priority. A job can issue a pointless I/O at 9.9 ms of every 10 ms slice and stay at the top forever. Fix (the new Rule 4): count the <strong>total allotment</strong> used at a level, whatever the I/O pattern, and demote the job once it is used up.</p>\n<p>(c) Without a boost, a stream of interactive jobs can <strong>starve</strong> long jobs, and a job that becomes interactive stays stuck low. Rule 5 moves every job to the top every S ms. If S is too small, CPU-bound jobs keep returning to the top and interactive jobs lose their advantage. If S is too large, long jobs starve. The value of S is a tuning choice (Ousterhout's &quot;voo-doo constant&quot;).</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p5","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p5","l":"P5","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p5\"><strong>P5.</strong> CFS with sched_latency = 48 ms and min_granularity = 6 ms. A and B have nice 0 (weight 1024), and C has nice −5 (weight 3121). (a) Give each process's time slice. [4] (b) Show how much each one's vruntime grows over one period. [3] (c) With 12 processes at nice 0, what slice does each get? [2] (d) Where does CFS put a process waking from a long sleep? [1]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) The total weight is 1024 + 1024 + 3121 = 5169. A and B each get 48 × 1024 / 5169 = <strong>9.51 ms</strong>, and C gets 48 × 3121 / 5169 = <strong>28.98 ms</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"math-d\" data-tex=\"\\text{slice}_k = \\frac{w_k}{\\sum w}\\cdot\\text{sched\\_latency}, \\qquad \\text{vruntime}_k \\mathrel{+}= \\frac{1024}{w_k}\\cdot\\text{runtime}_k\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8444em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">slice</span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:2.0436em;vertical-align:-0.936em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.1076em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mop op-symbol small-op\" style=\"position:relative;top:0em;\">∑</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0269em;\">w</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0269em;\">w</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0269em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.936em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1.0044em;vertical-align:-0.31em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">sched_latency</span></span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">vruntime</span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mord\">+</span></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.3669em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:2.1574em;vertical-align:-0.836em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0269em;\">w</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0269em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">1024</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.836em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8179em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">runtime</span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n<p>(b) A and B gain 9.51 × 1024/1024 = 9.51. C gains 28.98 × 1024/3121 = <strong>9.51</strong> too. vruntime advances equally, which is why weighted fairness works.</p>\n<p>(c) 48 / 12 = 4 ms, which is below min_granularity, so each gets <strong>6 ms</strong> (one round takes 72 ms).</p>\n<p>(d) CFS sets its vruntime to the <strong>minimum vruntime</strong> in the red-black tree. Otherwise its tiny vruntime would let it monopolise the CPU while it catches up.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p6","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p6","l":"P6","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p6\"><strong>P6.</strong> Multiprocessor scheduling. (a) What is cache affinity, and how does single-queue scheduling (SQMS) hurt it? [3] (b) Give two scalability problems with SQMS. [2] (c) Under MQMS, A is alone on CPU 0, while B and D share CPU 1. What is the problem, and how is it fixed? [3] (d) Why is cache coherence needed, and how does bus snooping provide it? [2]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) A process that runs on the same CPU again finds its data still in that CPU's cache, which makes it faster. SQMS gives each CPU whatever job is next in one global queue, so jobs bounce between CPUs and keep finding cold caches.</p>\n<p>(b) The single queue needs a <strong>lock</strong>, which becomes contended as the number of CPUs grows. There is also the affinity problem from (a), and extra mechanisms to fix it make SQMS more complex.</p>\n<p>(c) <strong>Load imbalance</strong>: A gets 100% of a CPU while B and D get 50% each. Fix it with <strong>migration</strong>, for example <strong>work stealing</strong>: a lightly loaded queue occasionally checks another queue and takes a job. Checking too often costs overhead, and too rarely leaves the imbalance.</p>\n<p>(d) Each CPU caches memory, so a write on one CPU can leave stale copies in the others. With <strong>bus snooping</strong>, every cache watches the shared bus and invalidates (or updates) its copy when it sees a write to that address.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p7","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p7","l":"P7","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p7\"><strong>P7.</strong> Network layers and devices. (a) Name the TCP/IP layers, the data unit at each, and the address each uses. [4] (b) Which header does a hub, a switch and a router each look at? [3] (c) A host sends a 1000-byte HTTP message over TCP/IP and Ethernet. Show the encapsulation, assuming 20-byte TCP and IP headers and an 18-byte Ethernet header and trailer. [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) <strong>Application</strong>: message, with hostnames and ports used by the app. <strong>Transport</strong>: segment (TCP) or datagram (UDP), with port numbers. <strong>Network</strong>: packet (datagram), with IP addresses. <strong>Link</strong>: frame, with MAC addresses. The physical layer below carries bits.</p>\n<p>(b) A <strong>hub</strong> works at layer 1 and reads no header: it repeats bits to every port. A <strong>switch</strong> works at layer 2: it reads the <strong>MAC</strong> address and forwards only to the right port, learning which port leads to which MAC. A <strong>router</strong> works at layer 3: it reads the <strong>IP</strong> address and forwards between networks using its routing table.</p>\n<p>(c) 1000 B of data, plus the TCP header = a 1020 B segment, plus the IP header = a 1040 B packet, plus the Ethernet header and trailer = a <strong>1058 B frame</strong>. Each layer treats everything above it as data.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p8","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p8","l":"P8","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p8\"><strong>P8.</strong> TCP. The client ISN is 1000 and the server ISN is 5000. (a) Give the seq and ack numbers of the three-way handshake. [3] (b) The client then sends 3 segments of 100 bytes. Give their seq numbers. The second segment is lost. What ACKs does the server send, and when does fast retransmit fire? [4] (c) Describe the 4-way close, and explain why TIME_WAIT exists. [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) SYN: seq = 1000. SYN-ACK: seq = 5000, ack = 1001. ACK: seq = 1001, ack = 5001. (A SYN uses up one sequence number.)</p>\n<p>(b) The segments have seq = 1001, 1101 and 1201. The server ACKs 1101 after the first. The second is lost. When the third arrives, the ACK is still <strong>1101</strong> (a duplicate), because ACKs are <strong>cumulative</strong>. Fast retransmit fires after <strong>three duplicate ACKs</strong> for 1101, which takes three more segments arriving after the gap. The sender then resends 1101 without waiting for the timeout.</p>\n<p>(c) The client sends FIN, and the server ACKs. Later the server sends its own FIN, and the client ACKs. The client then stays in <strong>TIME_WAIT</strong> (twice the maximum segment lifetime), so it can resend the last ACK if that is lost, and so delayed old segments cannot be mistaken for part of a new connection on the same ports.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p9","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p9","l":"P9","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p9\"><strong>P9.</strong> (a) EstimatedRTT = 80 ms and DevRTT = 8 ms. A SampleRTT of 120 ms arrives. Find the new EstimatedRTT, DevRTT and TimeoutInterval, with α = 0.125 and β = 0.25. [4] (b) ssthresh = 8 and cwnd starts at 1. List cwnd for rounds 1 to 8. Three duplicate ACKs arrive at the end of round 8. Give rounds 9 to 11. Then there is a timeout at the end of round 11. Give rounds 12 to 15. [6]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) EstimatedRTT = 0.875 × 80 + 0.125 × 120 = <strong>85</strong>. DevRTT = 0.75 × 8 + 0.25 × |120 − 85| = 6 + 8.75 = <strong>14.75</strong> (K&amp;R order: update the estimate first). Timeout = 85 + 4 × 14.75 = <strong>144 ms</strong>.</p>\n<p>(b)</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Round</th>\n<th>1</th>\n<th>2</th>\n<th>3</th>\n<th>4</th>\n<th>5</th>\n<th>6</th>\n<th>7</th>\n<th>8</th>\n<th>9</th>\n<th>10</th>\n<th>11</th>\n<th>12</th>\n<th>13</th>\n<th>14</th>\n<th>15</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>cwnd</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Slow start runs until round 4, when cwnd reaches ssthresh = 8. Congestion avoidance then adds 1 per round. After the 3 duplicate ACKs, ssthresh = 12 / 2 = 6 and cwnd = 6 (fast recovery, Reno). After the timeout, ssthresh = 8 / 2 = 4 and cwnd = 1, so slow start runs again (1, 2, 4) and then grows linearly from 4.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p10","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p10","l":"P10","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p10\"><strong>P10.</strong> (a) Compute the UDP checksum of the 16-bit words 0x1234, 0xF00F and 0x0F0F. [4] (b) List the socket calls for a TCP server and a TCP client, and say which ones block. [4] (c) Why does accept() return a new socket? [2]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) 0x1234 + 0xF00F = 0x10243. Wrap the carry around: 0x0243 + 1 = 0x0244. Then 0x0244 + 0x0F0F = 0x1153. The checksum is the ones' complement: <strong>0xEEAC</strong>. Check: 0x1153 + 0xEEAC = 0xFFFF.</p>\n<p>(b) <strong>Server</strong>: socket, bind, listen, accept (<strong>blocks</strong> until a client connects), recv (<strong>blocks</strong> until data arrives), send, close. <strong>Client</strong>: socket, connect (<strong>blocks</strong> for the handshake), send, recv (<strong>blocks</strong>), close. UDP has no listen, accept or connect: it uses sendto and recvfrom, and recvfrom blocks. A blocking call puts the process to sleep, so it is a context-switch point.</p>\n<p>(c) The listening socket keeps accepting new connections. Each accepted connection gets its own socket, identified by the 4-tuple (source IP, source port, destination IP, destination port). That is how one server on port 80 serves many clients at once.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p11","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p11","l":"P11","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p11\"><strong>P11.</strong> Process states (L02 s12-15). P0 needs 2 units of CPU, then issues an I/O that takes 3 units, then needs 1 more unit of CPU. P1 needs 4 units of CPU. P0 starts first. The OS switches to another process when the running one blocks, and a process that finishes its I/O becomes ready but does not preempt. (a) Give each process's state at times 1 to 7. [5] (b) How long would it take if the OS ran P1 only after P0 finished? [2] (c) Draw the state diagram, and say which transitions the OS triggers and which come from events. [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a)</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Time</th>\n<th>P0</th>\n<th>P1</th>\n<th>Note</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Running</td>\n<td>Ready</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Running</td>\n<td>Ready</td>\n<td>P0 issues the I/O at the end of this unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Blocked</td>\n<td>Running</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Blocked</td>\n<td>Running</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Blocked</td>\n<td>Running</td>\n<td>P0's I/O completes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Ready</td>\n<td>Running</td>\n<td>P1 finishes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Running</td>\n<td>Done</td>\n<td>P0 finishes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The CPU is busy for all 7 units.</p>\n<p>(b) 2 + 3 (CPU idle during the I/O) + 1 + 4 = <strong>10 units</strong>. Overlapping I/O with another process's computation saves 3 units.</p>\n<p>(c) Running → Ready is <strong>descheduled</strong> (OS, for example on a timer), and Ready → Running is <strong>scheduled</strong> (OS). Running → Blocked happens when the process starts I/O (its own call), and Blocked → Ready when the I/O completes (an interrupt). There is never a direct Blocked → Running edge.</p>\n"}];